# Topic prose and assessments authored by the primary assistant. Imported by the builder.
add('s4-g0-t0','dhcp-server','Allocate a lease from the intended scope without confusing allocation with connectivity.',
'A DHCPv4 server maintains address bindings and delivers configuration options to clients. A scope represents the intended subnet and allocatable range; exclusions protect infrastructure addresses, while reservations bind a chosen address to an identified client. The binding database is operational state, not merely a list of configured pools. This advanced lesson follows allocation, renewal, exhaustion and address-conflict evidence separately from the introductory DHCP exchange.',
['A selecting client broadcasts Discover, receives Offer, broadcasts Request identifying its choice and receives Ack if allocation is accepted. An Offer alone is not a committed lease. Record the client identifier, transaction and selected server; a MAC-only assumption can miss clients using a different identifier.',
'In the usual default timer case, T1 is half the lease and T2 is seven eighths. The bound client first renews toward its server, then rebinding can contact other servers. Explicit server timer options can override defaults. At lease expiry the client must stop using the address unless it obtained a valid extension.',
'A correctly selected pool can still be exhausted or administratively disabled. A valid lease can deliver incorrect gateway or DNS options. Inspect offered options and client bindings before diagnosing routing. Restore a deliberately exhausted disposable pool without deleting unrelated leases or starting a rogue server on an uplink.'],
'Define nonoverlapping subnet scopes, gateway/DNS options, exclusions, lease duration and intended client identifiers. Preserve binding state and conflict records before changing reservations. Use a supported redundancy design rather than assuming two independently overlapping pools form failover.',
'DHCPv4 provides no general client/server identity protection by itself. Limit trusted server/relay attachment, review snooping policy where supported and protect configuration backups containing reservations. A reserved address is not access authorization.',
'DHCPv4 uses UDP server67/client68. DHCPv6 uses UDP547/546 and has different message and relay behavior; this worksheet is DHCPv4.',
['show ip dhcp binding\nshow ip dhcp pool','Cisco IOS XE17 supported lab router','Compare committed client bindings and pool capacity; inspect scoped configuration separately.','A binding and available capacity must be correlated with actual client options and a valid lease.'],[rfc(2131)],
[['Scope matches client subnet','The allocatable pool corresponds to this client segment.','A wrong scope must be repaired at subnet selection; increasing its size does not fix selection.'],['Usable capacity remains','At least one nonexcluded usable address is available.','Exhaustion can suppress allocation despite a healthy server process.'],['Options match intended services','Supplied gateway and DNS options match the lab design.','A valid address with a wrong gateway or DNS still breaks the intended client experience.']],
[['Offer observed; may the client assume a committed lease?','No; complete the selecting exchange and obtain Ack.','An Offer advertises a proposed allocation; it is not the final acknowledgement of the chosen binding.',['Yes, Offer is a permanent allocation.','Yes, any broadcast commits every server.','Only the gateway commits leases.']],['A default eight-hour lease reaches T1; which action is expected?','Renew toward the allocating server at four hours.','T1 commonly defaults to half the lease; explicitly supplied timer values take precedence.'],['What is the default T2 for an eight-hour lease?','Seven hours.','Rebinding usually starts at seven eighths of the lease so another reachable server may extend it.'],['Client has a lease but names fail; what evidence comes next?','Inspect the delivered DNS option and query that resolver.','Address allocation and usable naming are separate; pool capacity does not establish correct resolver configuration.'],['Does a reservation authenticate the client?','No.','Client identifiers guide address selection; they do not establish a protected identity or authorization policy.'],['Pool is free but a gateway address is leased; what is missing?','An appropriate exclusion or controlled allocation policy.','Infrastructure addresses must be protected from dynamic allocation; an address conflict can exist before exhaustion.'],['Two overlapping independent servers are configured; is failover established?','No; use an explicit supported coordination design.','Independent overlapping allocation can create conflicts; redundancy needs binding coordination or a designed split scope.'],['Expired lease, no extension; may the address remain in use?','No; stop using that lease address.','An expired dynamic lease is no longer a valid permission to use the address.'],['What tradeoff comes with a shorter lease?','Faster reclamation with more renewal work and outage sensitivity.','Lease time affects allocation churn; it does not automatically make DNS or routing converge faster.'],['How do you demonstrate recovery from exhaustion?','Restore owned capacity, obtain a new valid lease and verify its options from the original client.','A pool display alone proves neither a completed exchange nor the delivered configuration.']])
add('s4-g0-t1','dhcp-relay','Carry DHCP across a router while preserving the client-subnet selection evidence.',
'A DHCPv4 relay forwards client messages between a broadcast domain and a remote server. It supplies relay context so the server can select the client subnet rather than the relay-to-server transit subnet. The giaddr field is central to conventional subnet selection and reply routing; Option82 can add attachment information under an explicitly defined trust policy. A relay is neither an address allocator nor a generic broadcast flood across the network.',
['A client broadcast reaches the relay on its client-facing interface. The relay supplies the appropriate giaddr and forwards toward a configured server. The server selects a pool using relay context rather than the IP source of the original unconfigured client.',
'The return must reach the relay and then the client using applicable message flags and client state. A working forward path is insufficient if ACLs, routing or relay handling break replies. Record server-side and client-side observations for the same transaction.',
'Option82 circuit/remote identifiers can refine server policy. Decide where they are inserted, whether untrusted received options are rejected or replaced, and where they are removed. Test incorrect giaddr separately from a missing return path; do not solve either by enlarging an unrelated scope.'],
'Attach the relay to the actual client L3 interface, identify the intended server and client subnet, and inspect helper forwarding behavior for the chosen release. Preserve helper addresses and option policies before one isolated change; restore exact interface attachment after testing.',
'Treat relay information as trusted only from controlled attachment points. Forged Option82 or unintended helper destinations can redirect allocation or disclose client metadata. Restrict forwarding to intended services and preserve a management recovery path.',
'Conventional DHCPv4 relay/server exchanges use UDP67; clients use UDP68. The exact forwarded and return tuples depend on client state and relay processing.',
['show running-config interface Vlan20\nshow ip route 192.0.2.10','Cisco IOS XE17 lab SVI; addresses are placeholders','Inspect helper attachment and the route to the owned server; obtain packet evidence separately.','Configured helper and route establish intent only, not giaddr correctness or a completed lease.'],[rfc(2131),['RFC3046 relay information option','https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc3046/']],
[['Correct relay subnet context','giaddr maps to the intended client subnet.','An incorrect giaddr can select the wrong pool even when the server is reachable.'],['Server accepts relay policy','Relay information is accepted under the defined trust policy.','A policy rejection is not the same as packet loss or an exhausted pool.'],['Reply reaches client segment','Server-to-relay and relay-to-client paths are supplied as ready.','Forward delivery does not prove the reply returned to the original client.']],
[['Why does a remote server need giaddr?','To identify the relayed client subnet and return relay context.','The unconfigured client cannot supply a routable source address that uniquely selects its pool.'],['Helper exists on the transit interface only; what is suspect?','The helper may be attached to the wrong client-facing L3 interface.','A configured destination is ineffective if the client broadcast never reaches that consuming interface.'],['Is Option82 itself a lease allocator?','No; it supplies relay information used by policy.','Allocation remains a server function, and the information must be trusted at the intended boundary.'],['Discover reaches server but no reply reaches client; where next?','Inspect server reply routing and relay-to-client handling.','A forward packet observation proves only that direction of the exchange.'],['Can the server choose the transit subnet pool from packet source?','That would ignore the required client-subnet relay context.','Use giaddr and supported relay selection policy rather than confusing transport source with client location.'],['Untrusted client supplies Option82; automatically accept it?','No; apply the designed reject/replace trust policy.','Attachment metadata can be forged; policy at the relay boundary determines what the server may trust.'],['Will larger wrong-subnet pools fix relay selection?','No; repair the subnet context.','Capacity and selection are independent; enlarging the wrong pool can hide a dangerous allocation error.'],['What confirms a restored relay?','The original client completes an exchange and receives intended subnet options.','Restored configuration should be corroborated by both transaction and client state.'],['Does relaying eliminate server capacity limits?','No.','A relay extends reach; allocation capacity and binding-state consistency remain server responsibilities.'],['What should a fault notebook identify?','Client interface, giaddr, transaction, server policy and both reply paths.','Observer and transaction identity prevent combining unrelated packets into a false success record.']],['s4-g0-t0'])
add('s4-g0-t2','dns-architecture','Separate authoritative truth, recursive lookup and cached answers.',
'DNS distributes naming data through delegated zones. Authoritative servers publish a zone; recursive resolvers pursue answers for clients and retain eligible results under TTL rules. A client stub usually asks a resolver rather than walking the hierarchy itself. A valid-looking cached answer can differ temporarily from newly changed authority, and a negative result can also be cached. DNS reachability, answer correctness and application delivery are different tests.',
['A recursive resolver checks applicable cache entries, then follows delegation or configured forwarding policy when it must resolve. Referral data points toward authority; an authoritative answer belongs to the responsible zone. Record query name, type, flags and responding server.',
'Positive TTL governs ordinary cache lifetime. Negative caching uses SOA information under RFC2308; NXDOMAIN means the queried name does not exist, while NOERROR with no relevant answer can mean the requested type is absent. Neither response is a silent timeout.',
'A changed zone record may coexist with an old cached answer until policy permits refresh. Compare authority and recursive views before clearing a local cache. DNSSEC validation checks signed-data authenticity under trust policy; it does not encrypt queries or guarantee the returned host runs the intended application.'],
'Keep authoritative publication and recursion exposure distinct. Plan delegation, redundancy, change ownership, zone serial/transfer policy and split-view behavior. Validate names and record types from intended client networks before shortening TTLs or editing forwarding.',
'Restrict open recursion, secure update/transfer permissions and protect resolver management. DNSSEC and encrypted resolver transport solve different problems. A DNS answer remains insufficient evidence of TLS identity or application authorization.',
'Traditional DNS uses UDP/TCP53; TCP is also needed in ordinary operation. Encrypted DNS transports have distinct ports and policy; this lesson does not deploy them.',
['dig @192.0.2.53 app.example.test A +norecurse','BIND9 client in an isolated lab','Ask the declared authority without requesting recursion; compare separately with the intended recursive resolver.','Inspect AA, status, answer type and TTL; a timeout differs from NXDOMAIN or a cached answer.'],[rfc(1034),rfc(2308)],
[['Authority has intended record','The responsible zone contains the intended name/type data.','Flushing a client cache cannot repair incorrect authoritative publication.'],['Resolver can obtain valid answer','The resolver path and applicable validation policy are supplied as ready.','A working authority does not ensure the client resolver can reach or validate it.'],['Client cache is current','The supplied client observation is not an old eligible cached result.','TTL and negative caching can explain different answers without an authoritative outage.']],
[['Who owns published zone data?','The authoritative zone operator.','A recursive resolver retrieves or caches data; its cache is not the source of zone ownership.'],['NXDOMAIN observed; is this the same as timeout?','No; it is an explicit negative DNS response.','An answer code is protocol evidence; absence of any response requires a separate path investigation.'],['NOERROR but no AAAA answer necessarily means name absent?','No; the name can exist without that record type.','Record type and response code must be interpreted together, including negative authority information.'],['Authority changed but resolver still returns old address; what next?','Compare cache TTL and authoritative answer at the same name/type.','Do not edit a correct zone merely because an eligible old cache result is still visible.'],['Does DNSSEC encrypt the query?','No.','DNSSEC validates signed data under a trust chain; confidentiality requires a separate transport mechanism.'],['Why permit TCP53 as well as UDP53?','Some DNS exchanges require or fall back to TCP.','Treating DNS as UDP-only can create failures for larger or otherwise TCP-dependent responses.'],['Can clearing local cache repair wrong authority?','No.','The next lookup can retrieve the same wrong source data; publication must be repaired at its owner.'],['How do you verify a negative-cache recovery?','Compare intended authority and recursive/client views after applicable expiry or controlled refresh.','Preserve the earlier negative response and SOA/TTL evidence rather than assuming immediate global visibility.'],['Why not set every TTL extremely low?','It increases query load and upstream dependency.','TTL changes trade cache reuse and change visibility; they do not supply redundant authority or better application health.'],['DNS resolves; what remains for HTTPS?','Connect to the intended service and validate TLS identity and response.','Naming is one dependency, not an end-to-end application transaction.']],['s1-g2-t1'])
add('s4-g0-t3','ntp','Estimate offset from four timestamps without mistaking stratum for accuracy.',
'NTP coordinates clocks using timestamp exchanges, sample filtering and source selection. In a simplified exchange T1 is client transmit, T2 server receive, T3 server transmit and T4 client receive. The ideal offset estimate is ((T2−T1)+(T3−T4))/2 and network delay is (T4−T1)−(T3−T2). These equations assume sufficiently symmetric path delay for an accurate offset estimate; a reachable low-stratum server is not automatically accurate or selected.',
['Identify the actual time source, synchronization status and observer clock before correlating events. Stratum describes hierarchy distance from a reference, not a measured accuracy guarantee. An unsynchronized server or unsuitable sample may be rejected despite a successful packet reply.',
'Compute offset and round-trip network delay from the four timestamps in common units. For T1=0,T2=12,T3=14,T4=10 milliseconds, offset is8ms and delay8ms. Unequal directional delay biases the offset; subtracting server processing time does not eliminate asymmetry.',
'A daemon filters samples and disciplines a clock; it does not simply assign every received timestamp. Observe selected source, offset trends and dispersion/root-distance evidence. Restore source policy in the isolated target rather than stepping a production clock to match one browser calculation.'],
'Define approved diverse sources, access policy, monitoring and time-dependent service requirements. Preserve source configuration before changing one owned peer. Validate synchronization and timestamp correlation over a suitable interval; do not force manual time changes on a production authentication system.',
'Unauthenticated time can be manipulated. Apply supported authenticated time mechanisms and management restrictions; confirm source identity and trust policy separately from reachability. Wrong time can break certificates, accounting correlation and replay-related checks.',
'NTP commonly uses UDP123. Authenticated time designs can add other transports; no clock is changed by this lesson.',
['chronyc tracking\nchronyc sources -v','Ubuntu24.04 with an already configured chrony lab daemon','Read synchronization and source-selection state without altering time.','Selected source, offset and synchronization evidence are distinct from merely seeing a reachable address.'],[rfc(5905)],
[['Source is synchronized','The declared source provides usable synchronized time.','A replying unsynchronized source must not be treated as a valid reference.'],['Sample passes acceptance policy','The declared sample meets configured clock-selection bounds.','Low stratum alone does not override unsuitable delay or uncertainty.'],['Observer confirms synchronization','The actual client synchronization observation is supplied.','Configured source intent is not evidence that the client selected or synchronized to it.']],
[['What does stratum measure?','Hierarchy distance from the reference clock.','Stratum does not directly certify accuracy, network symmetry or a trustworthy source.'],['Timestamps0,12,14,10ms: estimated offset?','8ms.','Average the two directional differences: (12+4)/2; this remains an ideal symmetry-dependent estimate.'],['Same timestamps: estimated network delay?','8ms.','Client elapsed10 minus server processing2 gives8; timestamp units and ordering must be consistent.'],['UDP reply received but client unsynchronized; is time verified?','No; inspect selection and synchronization state.','Protocol reachability is not the daemon decision to use a source.'],['Can path asymmetry bias estimated offset?','Yes.','The four-timestamp formula cannot independently identify unequal one-way delays from a single exchange.'],['Is manual clock stepping a harmless production diagnostic?','No; use a disposable clock target or read-only observations.','A time jump can affect authentication, logs and applications even when the arithmetic is correct.'],['Two logs disagree; what must be preserved?','Observer clock state, timezone and event/receive timestamps.','Correlation needs provenance rather than assuming every formatted timestamp shares one synchronized clock.'],['What demonstrates source-policy recovery?','Restored policy plus selected-source and client synchronization evidence.','A configured peer list alone does not show the discipline loop selected it.'],['Does a shorter poll interval guarantee better accuracy?','No.','Polling work, source quality, jitter and network asymmetry affect results; measure the actual clock behavior.'],['How should this arithmetic be reported?','An ideal timestamp estimate, separate from measured clock accuracy.','Browser calculations do not discipline a clock or execute the full NTP selection algorithm.']],['s4-g0-t2'])
add('s4-g1-t0','snmp','Read a scoped management object and distinguish polling from notifications.',
'SNMP exposes managed objects identified by OIDs through an agent and management framework. A successful request depends on endpoint reachability, security processing and access to the requested object/view. SNMPv3 security level authPriv adds authentication and privacy when correctly configured; an ordinary v1/v2c community does not offer equivalent protection. Polling counters and receiving notifications are distinct paths with different evidence.',
['Identify the engine, version, identity, security level and requested OID. SNMPv3 user security and view-based authorization are separate concerns: successful message authentication does not authorize every subtree. Capture errors without publishing credentials.',
'For counter observations, compare the same object and instance across a measured interval. A counter increment can estimate rate only when wrap/reset/discontinuity and units are understood.6000 new octets over60 seconds represents800bits/s, not6000bits/s.',
'A trap is normally an unacknowledged notification, while an inform includes acknowledgement behavior. Poll success does not prove notifications reach the receiver. Change one test OID view or one owned notification path and restore it before testing another condition.'],
'Prefer restricted read access under supported SNMPv3 authPriv configuration, dedicated management sources and narrowly selected objects. Use high-capacity counters where suitable, preserve instance mapping, and record poll interval and discontinuity evidence before computing utilization.',
'Protect authentication/privacy keys and management access; avoid putting secrets in saved shell history. Limit write access. Encryption on one management hop does not establish the trustworthiness or availability of a device itself.',
'Conventional polling uses UDP161 and notifications commonly UDP162. Other transports exist; the worksheet does not send SNMP messages.',
['show snmp user\nshow snmp group','Cisco IOS XE17 supported isolated device','Inspect configured user security and group/view bindings without displaying a secret-bearing client command.','A configured user/group is intent; verify a scoped OID query separately on the prepared target.'],[rfc(3411),rfc(3414)],
[['Security processing succeeds','The declared version/identity/security level is accepted.','An authentication failure is not resolved by widening an object view.'],['Requested OID is permitted','The exact object and instance are allowed by the supplied view.','A valid identity does not automatically authorize every OID.'],['Observation has continuity','Counter interval, instance and discontinuity context are valid.','A reset or changed instance invalidates a naive counter-difference rate.']],
[['Does an accepted SNMPv3 identity authorize every OID?','No; the configured view still governs access.','Security authentication and object authorization are separate processing decisions.'],['Is community-string SNMP equivalent to authPriv?','No.','Community-based access lacks the same message authentication/privacy protections offered by a properly configured v3 security model.'],['6000 octets over60s, stable counter: rate?','800bits/s.','Convert octets to bits and divide by elapsed seconds; continuity and interval must first be established.'],['Poll succeeds, collector sees no traps; what is missing?','Independent notification-path evidence.','Polling and notifications use different direction and receiver processing; one cannot establish the other.'],['Counter falls after reboot; use negative rate?','No; inspect reset/discontinuity before computing rate.','A counter discontinuity is not reverse traffic and cannot be treated as a normal monotonic difference.'],['What distinguishes an inform from a trap?','An inform uses acknowledgement behavior.','Acknowledgement semantics improve notification feedback but still require a separately working receiver path.'],['What does an OID instance identify?','A particular managed-object occurrence.','Comparing different interface instances can produce a meaningless rate even with valid authentication.'],['How do you test a restored view?','Query the exact intended OID and verify intended denial outside the view.','A positive result alone does not prove least-privilege boundaries remained intact.'],['Will polling every millisecond always improve monitoring?','No; it can overload agent and collector resources.','Sampling needs an interval matched to useful detection and device capacity, with actual observed latency.'],['How do you demonstrate a notification recovery?','Generate an owned test event and correlate agent and receiver observations.','Configured destination and poll results are not notification delivery evidence.']],['s4-g0-t3'])
add('s4-g1-t1','syslog','Preserve event provenance and verify collector delivery separately from local logging.',
'Syslog transports event messages from originators through optional relays to collectors. RFC5424 defines a structured message format including priority, timestamp, host/application identity and structured data; transport and framing are additional choices. The priority integer is facility times8 plus severity. Lower numerical severity is more urgent. A local log record, sender configuration and searchable collector record are separate observations.',
['Decode PRI into facility=floor(PRI/8) and severity=PRI modulo8. For local4 facility20 and warning severity4, PRI is164. Facility is classification, not a second urgency scale, and invalid priority values must not be silently accepted.',
'Preserve event time and receive time with timezone and clock status. A collector can receive a delayed record whose event timestamp is old. Legacy message variants can have incomplete provenance; never invent a year, timezone or authenticated sender from a displayed hostname.',
'UDP provides no transport delivery guarantee. TCP requires agreed framing; TLS can protect a connection under certificate-validation policy. Correlate an owned test marker at originator and collector, then verify filtering, forwarding, storage and retention separately.'],
'Choose facility/severity filters, secure collector paths, framing, retention and access controls. Save the exact sender and collector configuration before one test filter change. Verify redacted marker delivery and searchability after restoring; local logger success alone is insufficient.',
'Logs can contain secrets and untrusted fields. Apply transport authentication where supported, restrict query access and redact credential-bearing data. A sender-provided hostname or timestamp is not cryptographic identity or proof of correct time.',
'UDP514 is conventional Syslog; TLS transport conventionally TCP6514. TCP514 is implementation-dependent; framing must match the receiver.',
['show logging','Cisco IOS XE17 owned device','Inspect buffer, severity filters and declared destinations; preserve sensitive output privately.','A buffered local event does not prove network delivery, collector ingestion or retained search results.'],[rfc(5424)],
[['Sender filter admits marker','The test facility/severity is admitted by sender policy.','A dropped-by-filter event need never leave the originator.'],['Transport and framing match','The supplied collector transport/framing agrees with the sender.','A TCP connection alone does not establish correctly framed or accepted messages.'],['Collector stores correlated marker','The intended owned marker is observed in searchable storage.','Received bytes without retained searchable evidence do not complete this logging objective.']],
[['Facility20,severity4: PRI?','164.','Compute20×8+4; the priority encodes both classification and urgency.'],['Severity2 versus6: which is more urgent?','Severity2.','Syslog numerical severity decreases as urgency increases; facility does not reverse that scale.'],['Does a local buffer record prove collector delivery?','No.','Originator generation and remote transport/ingestion/storage are distinct evidence points.'],['TLS connected but records unparsable; what next?','Check message format and agreed framing.','A protected byte stream still needs application framing and parser agreement.'],['Old event time but current receive time; necessarily clock wrong?','No; delayed forwarding is another hypothesis.','Keep both timestamps and clock provenance rather than collapsing transport delay into one cause.'],['UDP send returned; does it confirm collector receipt?','No.','UDP transport provides no inherent acknowledgement of ingestion or storage.'],['Hostname in the message authenticates its author?','No.','A message field can be forged; trust requires separate origin and transport controls.'],['How do you verify filter recovery?','Send a uniquely correlated owned marker and find it at the intended collector.','An empty error list or restored configuration is weaker than observed end-to-end log evidence.'],['Why control severity volume and retention?','To balance diagnostic value, resource cost and exposure.','High-volume logging can exhaust buffers or storage and expose sensitive information without improving detection.'],['What must be preserved for incident ordering?','Event/receive times, timezone, clock state and origin identity evidence.','Timestamp text alone cannot establish a universal order across unsynchronized or delayed observers.']],['s4-g0-t3'])
add('s4-g2-t0','aaa','Treat authentication, authorization and accounting as distinct decisions.',
'AAA separates proving an identity, deciding which actions that identity may perform, and recording what occurred. A successful login does not automatically grant configuration permission, and a permitted command does not prove an accounting record was retained. Method lists and their attachments determine which policy applies to a console, virtual terminal or command path. Reject and unreachable are different outcomes that must not be collapsed into a permissive fallback.',
['Authentication checks the presented identity or credential using an attached method list. Record the entry point and service; configuring a server object without attaching the relevant policy may change nothing for that session.',
'Authorization evaluates the requested service, role or command independently. An explicit denial should remain a denial under the intended policy; local fallback on server error/timeout is a separately designed platform behavior, not permission to ignore rejection.',
'Accounting records starts, stops or command events according to policy. Maintain a tested console-local recovery account before changing central policy. Verify an approved identity, an intended denial and an unreachable-server recovery scenario separately; never destroy the recovery path to prove a lab fault.'],
'Define method list, consumer attachment, privilege scope, accounting destination and failure policy. Preserve the exact original console/VTY configuration and a tested independent local account. Attach experimental policy only to a disposable target or separately controlled session while console access remains.',
'Use least-privilege administration, secure credential storage, protected AAA transport and restricted management reachability. Prevent accidental fail-open behavior; accounting is detective evidence and does not replace authorization.',
'AAA is a framework with no intrinsic port. Its RADIUS and TACACS+ transports have their own conventions.',
['show aaa servers','Cisco IOS XE17 target where supported','Inspect server status alongside the exact consuming method list and session evidence.','A reachable server display does not prove authentication, command authorization or accounting success.'],[rfc(8907),rfc(2865)],
[['Authentication accepted','The supplied identity is authenticated for this service.','Explicit authentication rejection must not be relabelled as a server outage.'],['Requested action authorized','The supplied command or service is permitted for this identity.','Login success does not confer every administrative action.'],['Accounting record observed','A correlated record is retained at the intended observer.','Authorization success alone does not establish an audit trail.']],
[['Successful login means all configuration commands allowed?','No; authorization is separate.','A proved identity can still lack the role or privilege required for a requested action.'],['Reject and timeout interchangeable for fallback?','No.','Rejection is an explicit policy outcome; timeout is missing service evidence and may invoke a separately designed recovery method.'],['A permitted command proves an accounting record exists?','No.','Accounting generation, transport and retained storage must be verified independently of permission.'],['AAA server object exists but VTY uses local policy; what matters?','The actual method-list attachment.','A defined object is not necessarily consumed by the selected entry point or command service.'],['Should recovery be tested only after losing management access?','No; test the independent console-local path first.','A safe experiment establishes recovery before applying a central policy that can lock out administrators.'],['Denied user succeeds through fallback; desirable recovery?','No; investigate unintended fail-open policy.','Intended denial and unreachable-server recovery are separate acceptance cases.'],['What identity belongs in an accounting record?','The correlated authenticated identity and actual session/action context.','Uncorrelated records cannot reliably connect a command to its responsible administration session.'],['How do you validate restored AAA?','Test approved action, intended denial, accounting and independent recovery.','Positive login alone misses authorization leakage and unavailable audit evidence.'],['What causes latency under an unreachable server?','Configured timeout/retry sequencing and fallback behavior.','Method order and server responsiveness can affect access delay; measure the intended session rather than guessing.'],['What is the minimum change record?','Entry point, exact method list, server policy, original settings and tested rollback.','Consumer scope makes configuration changes reviewable without assuming every session follows the same policy.']],['s4-g1-t1'])
add('s4-g2-t1','radius','Interpret access policy and accounting without assuming classic RADIUS protects every field.',
'RADIUS carries network-access authentication/authorization information between a network access server and a policy server, with accounting defined separately. The NAS is the RADIUS client; the user endpoint is usually not the direct RADIUS peer. Access-Accept, Access-Reject and challenge processing are explicit responses. Classic UDP/shared-secret protection is not equivalent to modern full-channel encryption; current security and transport applicability must be checked for the chosen deployment.',
['The NAS sends an Access-Request for a defined service and identity context. Match source client configuration and secret securely, validate authenticators under the chosen protocol and apply identity policy. A response from an arbitrary address is not trustworthy evidence of an authorized server.',
'An Access-Accept can include policy attributes such as VLAN or role; the NAS must support and actually apply them. Acceptance does not establish that the resulting data-plane access matches intent. A reject remains distinct from timeout or challenge.',
'Accounting-Request/Response provides a separate event path with correlation identifiers. Test accept, explicit reject, malformed/untrusted response and accounting delivery separately. Use protected supported transport where appropriate; do not assume a classic shared secret encrypts all attributes.'],
'Configure owned NAS clients, source addresses, service policies and accounting endpoints. Preserve secret references privately and verify returned attributes at the consuming NAS. Use product-supported secure transport and current vulnerability guidance; no access server is configured here.',
'Classic RADIUS has important legacy cryptographic limits; validate authenticators and supported mitigations and prefer protected modern deployment options. Shared secrets must be unique and protected. Never interpret UDP reachability as trustworthy identity policy.',
'Conventional RADIUS authentication UDP1812 and accounting UDP1813; legacy deployments may differ. TLS-based transport has distinct port and configuration requirements.',
['show aaa servers','Cisco IOS XE17 lab NAS','Inspect the intended RADIUS peer status and correlate it with actual owned access/accounting transactions.','Access-Accept, installed policy and retained accounting are separate observations.'],[rfc(2865)],
[['NAS peer and response trusted','Declared client/server context and response validation are correct.','An untrusted response must not be treated as an authorized Access-Accept.'],['Access policy accepts identity','The supplied access decision is Accept for the intended service.','Reject is explicit denial; timeout cannot be silently substituted for it.'],['Returned policy is applied','The NAS applies the intended authorized policy attributes.','Server acceptance alone does not prove the desired access VLAN or role took effect.']],
[['Who normally acts as the RADIUS client?','The NAS or authenticator.','The user endpoint usually interacts with the access device rather than being the direct policy-server peer.'],['Classic shared secret encrypts every RADIUS attribute?','No.','Legacy attribute protection and authenticator mechanisms do not provide general modern encrypted-channel semantics.'],['Access-Accept guarantees intended VLAN installed?','No; inspect the consuming NAS policy.','A returned attribute needs supported interpretation and actual attachment on the access device.'],['Access-Reject received; should it be called timeout?','No.','A validated reject is a server policy decision, not absence of server response.'],['Which conventional port handles accounting?','UDP1813.','Authentication commonly uses1812; the accounting exchange is a separately configured function.'],['Response arrives from wrong peer; permit access?','No; validate trusted server context and authenticator.','Transport arrival does not establish authenticity of an access-policy decision.'],['Can successful authentication replace accounting verification?','No.','Accounting generation, delivery and retention require their own correlated evidence.'],['How do you test restoration after an attribute fault?','Restore policy, repeat owned access and inspect the resulting NAS role/VLAN.','A policy-server configuration display is not proof of applied client access.'],['Why bound timeout and retry settings?','To balance failover detection and access delay.','Excessive retries can prolong unavailable service; overly aggressive limits can misclassify transient delay.'],['What separates access success from usable service?','Applied policy and the original allowed/denied application tests.','Even correct authentication can coexist with wrong authorization or a separate network path failure.']],['s4-g2-t0'])
add('s4-g2-t2','tacacs','Follow device administration authentication and command authorization separately.',
'TACACS+ supports device-administration authentication, authorization and accounting using separate protocol exchanges over TCP. A device can authenticate an administrator and later request authorization for a command and arguments. The legacy shared-secret packet-body mechanism is obfuscation based on an older construction; it does not provide modern full-channel encryption, and the protocol header remains visible. Its transport reliability does not prove the requested command is permitted or audited.',
['Identify the actual administrative entry point and attached policy. Authentication proves the presented identity for a session; subsequent authorization may carry service, command and arguments. Read the explicit authorization result without replacing denial with a guessed server error.',
'TCP delivery and a shared secret are transport prerequisites. Secret mismatch or server unreachability differs from an authenticated explicit denial. A configured server group can be bypassed by a different method-list attachment on another line.',
'Accounting records must be correlated with the actual action and retained receiver evidence. Keep console-local recovery independent and test it before changing command authorization. Restrict legacy TACACS+ to a protected management path under supported security controls.'],
'Choose administration roles, command/argument policy, intended line attachments and accounting events. Preserve the current privilege and recovery design before adding a policy to a controlled lab session. Validate approved command, denied command and unavailable-server recovery separately.',
'Do not describe legacy body obfuscation as TLS-equivalent protection. Protect the management network and secrets, prefer supported stronger channel options where available, and constrain local recovery to an explicitly tested administrative path.',
'TACACS+ conventionally uses TCP49. Legacy protocol body protection does not hide its header or replace modern channel security.',
['show tacacs','Cisco IOS XE17 lab device where this observation is supported','Inspect server statistics and separately preserve command authorization/accounting outcomes.','Transport counters and server status do not prove a command was allowed or its record retained.'],[rfc(8907)],
[['Administration session authenticated','The declared administrator identity has been accepted.','A TCP connection is not an authenticated administrator session.'],['Command authorization permits action','The actual command/argument request is permitted.','An authenticated session may correctly be denied a configuration command.'],['Accounting corroborates action','A correlated action record is observed at the accounting receiver.','A permitted command without a retained record leaves this audit objective incomplete.']],
[['Conventional TACACS+ transport?','TCP49.','A reliable byte stream does not by itself establish identity, permission or retained accounting.'],['Does legacy TACACS+ encrypt the entire packet like TLS?','No.','The legacy body mechanism is obfuscation; visible headers and outdated protection must be acknowledged.'],['Authenticated user denied command; is authentication broken?','No; authorization is a separate result.','Successful identity validation can coexist with the intended denial of a requested administrative action.'],['Server reachable but command bypasses policy; inspect what?','The consuming command-authorization method list and line attachment.','Defined server objects do not govern an action unless the selected service consumes them.'],['Explicit denial should trigger unrestricted local permit?','No.','Failover for server error is a separate design and must not erase a valid policy denial.'],['What context matters in command authorization?','Command, arguments, service and authenticated identity.','Authorizing a command name without relevant context may differ from the intended privilege boundary.'],['Does a transport counter prove accounting retention?','No.','Records require correlation and receiver-side storage evidence rather than only transmitted-message counts.'],['What recovery path must be tested first?','Independent console-local administration.','The recovery mechanism must remain usable before attaching a policy that could deny remote management.'],['What affects login delay under an unreachable server?','Retry/timeout and method-list order.','TCP behavior and policy failover together affect latency; no browser preset measures those delays.'],['How do you demonstrate complete restored administration?','Approved action, intended denial, accounting and console recovery all tested.','A login-only positive check overlooks command permission and audit-path failures.']],['s4-g2-t0'])
add('s4-g3-t0','netflow','Decode exported flow records with the matching template and observation context.',
'NetFlow exports summaries of observed traffic, not a packet capture. In version9, templates describe data-record fields and must be known to the collector for the relevant exporter/source context. Cache timeouts determine when active or inactive flows are exported; sampling changes what the counts represent. Export traffic can arrive at a collector that still cannot decode it because its template or context is missing.',
['Define flow keys and observation point, direction and cache behavior. A bidirectional application may appear as separate directional flow records. Flow records omit packet payload and cannot substitute for a capture when diagnosing exact retransmissions or content.',
'A version9 template identifies the record schema in the exporter/source context. Refresh and collector restart behavior matter; template IDs cannot be assumed globally unique across exporters. A data record without its matching template is not safely interpretable by guessing field positions.',
'Interpret timestamps, byte/packet units, sampling and active/inactive export boundaries. A sampled count needs its documented sampling semantics before population estimation. Test collector restart/template refresh and restore the same observation point without interpreting missing export as zero traffic.'],
'Specify exporter source, collector address/port, version, interface direction, cache timeouts and sampling. Record collector receipt and decoded fields with source/template identity. Protect template refresh and monitoring continuity during upgrades or collector restart.',
'Flow metadata exposes communication patterns and may include sensitive identities. Restrict exporters/collector access, retain data under policy and do not treat observed source addresses as authenticated endpoint identities.',
'NetFlow export destination UDP port is configured by deployment, not universally fixed. NetFlowv9 is distinct from IPFIX; this lesson uses v9 template semantics.',
['show ip cache flow','Cisco IOS XE17 platform supporting the selected traditional NetFlow feature','Read cache summaries at the declared observation point; this command is not a portable FNF cache substitute.','A local flow cache is distinct from correctly decoded collector records.'],[rfc(3954)],
[['Observation covers intended traffic','The selected interface/direction actually observes the traffic.','No matching observation can result from wrong attachment rather than absent application traffic.'],['Matching template available','The collector has the record schema for this exporter/source context.','Received data cannot be reliably decoded using another exporter template with the same numeric ID.'],['Sampling context recorded','The supplied counters have known sampling and interval semantics.','Unknown sampling prevents a defensible total-traffic estimate.']],
[['Are exported flows a full packet capture?','No; they summarize selected traffic properties.','Payload and individual packet timing may be absent even when flow counters are correct.'],['Why can received version9 data remain undecodable?','Its matching template may be missing.','Record bytes require the appropriate schema and exporter/source context.'],['Template ID256 from two exporters necessarily same schema?','No.','Template identity includes exporter/source context rather than only a global numeric ID.'],['Collector restarts and loses schema; what should be checked?','Template refresh and collector state for that exporter.','Data reception alone cannot recover a missing schema by safely guessing field positions.'],['One directional flow proves replies exist?','No.','Flow direction and observation point must be compared with opposite-direction evidence.'],['Unknown sampling; can count be called total packets?','No.','Counts need documented sampling semantics and observation intervals before total estimation.'],['Why might active flows export before they end?','An active cache timeout can export periodic records.','Export boundaries are cache-policy artifacts, not necessarily application transaction boundaries.'],['What demonstrates restored collector decoding?','Matching template and correlated decoded records at the original observation point.','A UDP listener alone cannot prove fields were interpreted correctly.'],['What changes with short active timeouts?','More frequent records and collector/export overhead.','Time granularity trades resource cost; it does not create packet-level visibility.'],['How do you distinguish no traffic from lost telemetry?','Use independent observation plus exporter/collector/template evidence.','Missing records alone cannot distinguish inactivity, wrong attachment, export loss and decoding failure.']],['s4-g1-t0'])
# Flexible flow, availability, discovery and file-transfer topics.
add('s4-g3-t1','fnf','Distinguish flow keys, collected fields, monitor attachment and export.',
'Flexible NetFlow separates a flow record definition, a cache monitor and an exporter. Match fields identify distinct flows; collect fields add measurements or metadata without necessarily splitting the key. The monitor attaches the record and cache behavior to an observation point, and an exporter sends records to a collector. Hardware and software support for fields, directions and combinations vary by platform and release.',
['Select match fields to define the aggregation identity. Adding source port as a match field can split a previous aggregate into more entries. Adding a supported collected counter changes recorded values rather than inherently creating a new key.', 'Attach the record to a monitor and the monitor to a supported interface/direction. An unattached monitor does not observe the intended traffic. Confirm platform limitations before assuming a configuration accepted elsewhere works on this device.', 'Associate an exporter, then correlate cache records, template availability and collector decoding. Successful local aggregation does not demonstrate export; successful export receipt does not demonstrate schema or interval interpretation.'],
'Preserve record/monitor/exporter definitions and exact attachment. Plan cardinality, supported fields and timeouts. Change only an owned monitor on a disposable target; verify key changes through correlated cache entries and collector records, then restore the prior attachment.',
'Limit collector access and sensitive flow fields. A record definition is a visibility policy, not a traffic firewall or endpoint authentication mechanism.',
'Exporter destination port and transport depend on configuration; NetFlowv9/IPFIX formats are not interchangeable merely because they carry flow data.',
['show flow record\nshow flow monitor\nshow flow exporter','Cisco IOS XE17 FNF-supported lab platform','Inspect record keys/collect fields, monitor cache and exporter independently.','Each display answers a different question; correlate actual interface attachment and collector decoding.'],[['Cisco IOS XE17 Flexible NetFlow','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/routers/ios/config/17-x/ntw-servs/b-network-services/m_fnf-fnetflow.html']],
[['Record supported and appropriate','Selected match/collect fields are supported and reflect intended aggregation.','Unsupported fields or unintended keys invalidate the planned aggregation.'],['Monitor attached at observer','The monitor is applied to the intended supported interface/direction.','Configured monitor objects do not observe traffic before attachment.'],['Exporter and decoder ready','Supplied export and matching collector schema are ready.','A populated local cache alone does not prove decoded collector visibility.']],
[['Does a collect field always split the flow key?','No.','Match fields define identity; collect fields record additional values under the selected record semantics.'],['Adding source port as match can change what?','Flow cardinality and aggregation boundaries.','Traffic previously combined under one key can be divided into several entries.'],['Record exists but cache empty; next attachment check?','Monitor on the intended interface and direction.','A defined record without an active consumer sees no packets at that observation point.'],['Cache populated but collector blank; where next?','Exporter path, template and collector decoding.','Observation and local caching precede distinct export/ingestion stages.'],['Are FNF fields universally supported on all IOS XE devices?','No.','Hardware, release, license and observation feature combinations constrain support.'],['Can a flow monitor replace an ACL?','No.','Observation summarizes traffic; it does not inherently enforce a permit/deny policy.'],['Exporter configured means records received?','No.','Sender intent must be corroborated at the collector and with matching record schema.'],['What verifies a restored record key?','Equivalent traffic produces intended cache aggregation and correctly decoded fields.','Configuration text alone does not prove the changed key is being consumed.'],['Why avoid unnecessary match fields?','They can increase cache cardinality and resource work.','More detailed keys trade aggregation and capacity; they do not automatically improve detection quality.'],['What rollback is precise?','Restore the prior record/monitor/exporter associations and consuming attachment.','Removing a named object without restoring its consumer can leave monitoring absent.']],['s4-g3-t0'])
add('s4-g3-t2','ip-sla','Read an active probe result in its actual source and operation context.',
'Cisco IP SLA performs configured active measurements such as ICMP echo or protocol-specific transactions on supported platforms. Each operation has a source, destination, timing and success interpretation. A probe measures that operation from that observer; a successful ping is not proof of an authenticated application workflow. Probe scheduling and return-code evidence are separate from merely defining an operation.',
['Choose an operation that measures the intended dependency. ICMP echo observes an echo exchange; application operations have different semantics and support. Pin source/interface/VRF where relevant so the observation represents the intended path.', 'Confirm the operation is scheduled, record timeout/frequency and inspect return code and latency at a known time. A stale successful sample should not be treated as current service evidence. Missing schedule and failed transaction are different diagnoses.', 'Tracking can consume selected SLA outcomes, but the probe itself changes no route merely by existing. Preserve measurement reachability when a dependent route withdraws so recovery remains observable; avoid a circular path dependency.'],
'Document operation type, source context, target, schedule, expected outcome and dependent consumer. Preserve probe reachability and management access. Verify baseline/fault/recovery from the same operation rather than switching to an easier target after failure.',
'Bound probe rates and use owned targets. Active probes can be filtered or manipulated; they do not authenticate the whole service or authorize routing changes without an explicit consumer.',
'Ports/protocol depend on the chosen operation: ICMP has no TCP/UDP port; TCP or UDP operations use their configured target service.',
['show ip sla configuration\nshow ip sla statistics','Cisco IOS XE17 IP SLA-supported lab target','Compare operation source/schedule and current result code.','A successful ICMP operation is evidence of that echo exchange, not application health.'],[['Cisco IOS XE IP SLA guide','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/routers/ios/config/17-x/ip-addressing/b-ip-addressing/m_sla_overview.html']],
[['Operation represents intended path','Type, source and routing context match the desired measurement.','A probe from another source or VRF may observe a different path.'],['Schedule and sample current','The operation runs and the supplied result is current.','A defined but unscheduled operation has no fresh measurement.'],['Measured operation succeeds','The supplied result code indicates this operation succeeded.','This failure is scoped to the probe; this success is not general application proof.']],
[['ICMP SLA succeeds; authenticated web workflow verified?','No.','Echo reply and a protected application transaction test different dependencies.'],['Operation defined but never scheduled; result expected?','No fresh measurement.','Definition and execution schedule are independent configuration requirements.'],['Why specify source or VRF?','To measure the intended observer and path.','A different source may follow another route and policy, hiding the original failure.'],['Old success display while schedule stopped; conclusion?','Insufficient current evidence.','A timestamped historical sample is not a current measurement of availability.'],['Does creating an SLA operation change routes automatically?','No.','An explicit tracking/routing consumer is required to act on its results.'],['What can prevent recovery detection after route withdrawal?','The probe depends on the very route its failure removes.','Maintain an independently valid measurement path or an explicitly designed recovery mechanism.'],['Is every operation supported on every platform?','No.','Release, hardware and feature support must be verified before applying the reference procedure.'],['How do you verify restored probe behavior?','Restore the owned cause and observe fresh success from the original operation/source.','Changing the source or target can make a different probe pass without repairing the original path.'],['What tradeoff does higher probe frequency create?','Faster sampling with more traffic and processing.','Frequency is not convergence time; timeout and consumer delays also matter.'],['What belongs in an incident record?','Operation, source/VRF, target, sample time, return code and consumer state.','That scope prevents a narrow probe result being promoted to a universal service claim.']],['s4-g0-t3'])
add('s4-g3-t3','tracking','Convert scoped observations into delayed state without losing the recovery path.',
'Object tracking exposes a state derived from an interface, route, IP SLA result or supported logical combination to a consuming feature. Tracking does not itself create a route, elect a gateway or test an application; those effects depend on the consumer. Delay-up and delay-down can hold a state transition to reduce flapping. A configuration and a current accepted state are separate observations.',
['Define the tracked object and its underlying measurement. The meaning of up/down follows that object type; interface line protocol, prefix reachability and an SLA return code are not interchangeable application tests.', 'A delay policy can keep the accepted state unchanged until the new condition persists sufficiently. Up delay and down delay are independent. Check sample timing and pending state before assuming one failed probe immediately withdrew a route.', 'Identify the routing or redundancy consumer and keep measurement reachability independent where necessary. If withdrawal removes the probe path, restoring the remote service may not produce a new success. Restore the actual owned path before claiming recovery.'],
'Record object identity, type, delay policy and every consumer. Preserve exact tracked-route attachment and ensure probe/management reachability. Verify the timeline of measurement, accepted state and consumer action rather than relying on one instantaneous display.',
'Restrict configuration changes and protect management during intentional down states. A spoofable measurement can influence an availability decision; match the tracked condition to a justified dependency.',
'Tracking has no intrinsic transport port. Its underlying IP SLA or service measurement determines network traffic.',
['show track\nshow ip route 198.51.100.0/24','Cisco IOS XE17 enhanced tracking lab; prefix placeholder','Read object type, state/delay and the actual consuming route separately.','Object up does not prove consumer installation, forwarding or the original application transaction.'],[['Cisco IOS XE17 enhanced object tracking','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst9300/software/release/17-15/configuration_guide/ip/b_1715_ip_9300_cg/configuring_enhanced___object_tracking.html']],
[['Underlying observation valid','The supplied measurement belongs to the intended object and context.','A mismatched probe cannot safely govern the intended route.'],['Delay policy satisfied','The new condition has persisted through the configured transition delay.','One changed sample may not immediately change the accepted track state.'],['Consumer and recovery path valid','The consumer is attached and measurement can still detect recovery.','A route withdrawn by its own dependent probe can prevent later recovery observations.']],
[['Does a tracking object install a route by itself?','No.','A consuming routing or redundancy feature must be explicitly attached.'],['Down sample during delay-down guarantees immediate withdrawal?','No.','The accepted state can remain unchanged until transition timing satisfies policy.'],['Is interface-up proof of application health?','No.','Line protocol state has a narrower meaning than a verified application transaction.'],['Track up but route absent; inspect what?','The actual consumer attachment and route eligibility.','A valid object state is only an input to the consuming feature.'],['Why separate up and down delays?','Recovery and failure stabilization may need different durations.','Delay choices affect state timing, not the semantic meaning of the underlying observation.'],['Probe route removed by its own tracking failure; danger?','Recovery can become unobservable.','The measurement needs a path that remains valid or an explicit recovery design.'],['Can logical object combinations be assumed universal?','No; inspect the supported type and exact boolean policy.','AND/OR or threshold semantics depend on configured feature support and consumer intent.'],['How verify restoration?','Observe fresh measurement, accepted track state, consumer state and original transaction.','A restored checkbox or configuration does not establish all downstream effects.'],['Does a longer delay always increase availability?','No; it can prolong use of a failed dependency.','Stability and detection time trade off; use actual workload and recovery evidence.'],['What timeline should be recorded?','Sample time, pending transition, accepted state and consumer action.','A single display can miss the delay between observation and operational effect.']],['s4-g3-t2'])
add('s4-g4-t0','cdp','Use neighbor advertisements as local attachment clues, with explicit trust limits.',
'Cisco Discovery Protocol advertises device and interface information to directly connected participating neighbors at Layer2. A neighbor table supplies topology clues such as device identity, port and capabilities. It does not prove IP routing, endpoint authentication or an application reply. Information may remain until its holdtime expires, and a lack of advertisements can reflect disabled discovery rather than a broken link.',
['Identify the local receiving interface and remote advertised device/port. CDP information is scoped to a local adjacency and should be compared with physical or virtual attachment evidence. Devices that do not implement or enable CDP need not appear.', 'Interpret advertised management addresses and capabilities as supplied metadata. They do not establish a reachable management service or authorized endpoint. A stale entry can outlive a failed or changed attachment until aging removes it.', 'Change discovery only on an owned lab interface, compare before/fault/after neighbor evidence and restore it. Verify the original service separately; do not bounce links just to refresh a topology hypothesis.'],
'Enable discovery only where its operational value fits the exposure policy. Preserve interface-level and global state before a lab disable/restore. Correlate local port, remote advertised port and actual patch/virtual-link mapping.',
'CDP reveals infrastructure metadata and its advertisements are not endpoint authentication. Restrict untrusted-edge exposure and treat received names/addresses as untrusted input.',
'CDP is a Layer2 discovery protocol with no TCP/UDP service port; it does not route across ordinary L3 boundaries.',
['show cdp neighbors detail\nshow cdp interface','Cisco IOS XE17 CDP-supported lab device','Read neighbor metadata and local advertisement status.','A neighbor entry is an advertised local attachment clue, not verified routed management access.'],[['Cisco IOS XE CDP configuration','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/routers/ios/config/17-x/syst-mgmt/b-system-management/m_nm-cdp-discover-xe.html']],
[['Local discovery enabled','The receiving lab interface participates in CDP.','No entry with discovery disabled does not establish physical link failure.'],['Advertisement is current','The supplied entry remains valid in its holdtime context.','A stale cached advertisement can survive a failed attachment temporarily.'],['Attachment corroborated independently','Port mapping is compared with separate owned topology evidence.','Advertised identity alone is not authenticated physical or service evidence.']],
[['CDP uses TCP or UDP?','Neither; it operates at Layer2.','A TCP/UDP port list cannot describe local CDP neighbor discovery.'],['Neighbor advertises an IP; routing verified?','No.','An advertised address is metadata, not an observed routed transaction.'],['No neighbor with CDP disabled means cable broken?','No.','Discovery participation is a prerequisite separate from carrier and cabling evidence.'],['Link changed but old neighbor remains; why possible?','Holdtime aging can retain prior information temporarily.','Neighbor state must be interpreted with observation time and refresh/expiry context.'],['Does an advertised name authenticate the device?','No.','Received metadata can be forged or misleading and needs an independent trust mechanism.'],['Where should discovery exposure be restricted?','Untrusted attachment points where metadata disclosure is unnecessary.','Topology assistance should be balanced with identity/address disclosure policy.'],['Does CDP traverse an ordinary router hop?','No.','Its local link scope differs from routed management protocols.'],['How verify restored discovery?','Observe fresh intended neighbor on the same local interface after restore.','A global enable flag alone does not establish interface participation or a received advertisement.'],['Why not infer throughput from neighbor capability?','Capabilities are advertisements rather than traffic measurements.','Operational bandwidth and application performance require separate observations.'],['What is the useful topology record?','Local port, remote advertised port, observation time and corroborated attachment.','Combining names without observer context can connect unrelated interfaces incorrectly.']],['s2-g0-t0'])
add('s4-g4-t1','lldp','Read standardized local-link discovery without treating a neighbor table as authorization.',
'LLDP is standardized local-link discovery using advertisements containing chassis and port identifiers and additional optional TLVs. It helps map adjacent interfaces across participating vendors, while optional extensions can convey endpoint-specific information. Advertising and receiving can be independently controlled by implementations. A management-address TLV is not proof of a reachable host, and discovery does not grant network access.',
['Interpret chassis ID subtype and port ID subtype rather than assuming both are friendly names or MAC addresses. Preserve the receiving interface and TTL with the advertised values; optional system or management fields may be absent.', 'A neighbor record remains subject to advertisement refresh and TTL expiry. Missing records can indicate disabled transmission/reception, unsupported participation or a link problem. Compare local LLDP status and independent attachment evidence before choosing a cause.', 'Inspect extension applicability and trust separately from base LLDP. A phone-related TLV does not prove voice VLAN/QoS configuration was accepted. Restore only the owned participation settings and verify fresh advertisements at the same observer.'],
'Plan per-interface transmit/receive participation and metadata exposure. Save chassis/port/TTL evidence before one controlled change. Validate cross-vendor field interpretation and corroborate any management or endpoint policy claims independently.',
'Advertisements expose topology and may contain untrusted identifiers. Limit unnecessary edge exposure and do not use neighbor presence as authentication or a replacement for 802.1X.',
'LLDP uses local Layer2 frames rather than a TCP/UDP service port. It does not prove routed reachability.',
['show lldp neighbors detail\nshow lldp interface','Cisco IOS XE17 LLDP-supported target','Compare per-interface participation, TTL and neighbor TLVs.','Neighbor fields identify advertised context; validate actual attachment and service separately.'],[['IEEE802.1AB standard overview','https://standards.ieee.org/standard/802_1AB-2016.html']],
[['Participation matches direction','The relevant transmitter and receiver participate.','A disabled receive path can hide a healthy link advertisement.'],['Neighbor TTL is current','The advertised state is valid at this observation time.','An aged or stale entry is not a fresh attachment observation.'],['Identifier context understood','Chassis/port subtype and separate topology evidence are supplied.','Assuming every ID is a MAC address can misidentify the neighbor.']],
[['Are chassis and port IDs always MAC addresses?','No; interpret identifier subtypes.','LLDP identifiers support multiple forms, so subtype is part of the observation.'],['A management-address TLV proves SSH works?','No.','The field is advertised metadata rather than a protected routed transaction.'],['Transmit enabled, receive disabled; local table guaranteed?','No.','Sending and receiving participation can be independently configured.'],['No LLDP neighbor; first evidence before link reset?','Participation, refresh/TTL and independent link/attachment state.','Discovery absence has several causes beyond physical failure.'],['Does LLDP grant network authorization?','No.','Topology advertisement is separate from identity-based access control.'],['Can a phone TLV prove voice QoS applied?','No.','Advertised information and actual endpoint/port behavior need separate verification.'],['What does TTL govern?','Lifetime of advertised neighbor information.','Expiry relates to table freshness, not a universal service timeout.'],['How verify restore?','Fresh intended TLVs received on the original local interface.','Configuration intent should be corroborated by current observer data.'],['Does LLDP measure application bandwidth?','No.','Local discovery describes attachment metadata rather than actual traffic performance.'],['What makes a defensible neighbor record?','Receiver port, ID subtypes, TTL/time and independent attachment evidence.','Names alone can be ambiguous across interfaces and reused devices.']],['s4-g4-t0'])
add('s4-g4-t2','ssh','Verify the server host before authenticating the administrative user.',
'SSH establishes a protected remote-session transport, authenticates server host identity and supports user authentication and channels. Host identity and user identity answer different questions. A changed host key can indicate a legitimate rebuild or an interception problem; verify it through a trusted independent channel before accepting replacement. A successful encrypted connection does not authorize every remote command or prove the intended server was verified.',
['The transport negotiates algorithms and proves possession of the server host key. Client trust policy compares that key with a known or certified identity. Encryption to an unverified endpoint does not establish that it is the intended device.', 'User authentication occurs within the protected transport using supported credentials. Public-key authentication proves possession of an authorized private key; it does not mean the private key should be copied to the server. Authorization remains a server/session policy.', 'Channels carry shell, execution, forwarding or subsystem traffic according to policy. Bound management access and forwarding features. Test an intended host, an intentional wrong-key case and an approved/denied user without disabling host verification.'],
'Preserve known host trust and server access policy, use dedicated administrative identities and restrict unnecessary forwarding. Confirm host fingerprints through an independent trusted path; restore precise trust records only after identity verification.',
'Do not use StrictHostKeyChecking=no as a recovery shortcut. Protect private keys, use supported modern algorithms and limit session privileges. A copied fingerprint from the same untrusted connection is not independent verification.',
'SSH conventionally uses TCP22; configured nondefault ports are possible. Host verification remains required regardless of the port.',
['ssh -G admin@lab.example.test','OpenSSH client; configuration expansion only','Inspect effective client settings without connecting to a host.','Expanded settings show intent, not a verified server, accepted credential or executed session.'],[['OpenSSH manual pages','https://www.openssh.org/manual.html'],rfc(4253)],
[['Host identity verified','The expected host key or host certificate passes trusted policy.','An encrypted channel to an unverified key does not identify the intended server.'],['User authentication accepted','The supplied administrative user proves an accepted credential.','Trusted server identity does not automatically authenticate a user.'],['Requested channel permitted','The server policy permits the intended shell/action/subsystem.','A successful login need not permit forwarding or every command.']],
[['Does encryption alone verify the intended SSH server?','No; host identity must be verified.','A protected connection can still terminate at an unintended endpoint under permissive trust policy.'],['Where should a private authentication key normally remain?','With its protected owner, not copied to the server.','The server can verify authorized public-key possession without receiving the private key.'],['Changed host key; immediate trust deletion appropriate?','No; verify the change independently first.','A rebuild and interception can present similar symptoms, so the trusted identity evidence decides the response.'],['ssh -G succeeded; remote session verified?','No; it expands local configuration.','No network authentication or remote command takes place in this read-only inspection.'],['User authenticated; forwarding automatically allowed?','No.','Channel authorization and restrictions remain separate from identity authentication.'],['What is the conventional SSH port?','TCP22.','Changing a port does not replace host trust, authentication or least privilege.'],['Fingerprint learned only from suspect session is independent?','No.','Trust must come from a separate authenticated source rather than the same possible interception path.'],['How verify access recovery?','Trusted host identity, approved user action and intended denial verified.','A reconnect with disabled host checking would hide the original identity problem.'],['What limits concurrent session impact?','Server capacity and policy, measured under the intended workload.','An open port does not establish session responsiveness or a sustainable administrative workload.'],['What does a safe key-change record include?','Trusted change evidence, expected fingerprint and exact affected host entry.','Broad known-host deletion removes unrelated trust and weakens later verification.']],['s4-g2-t0'])
add('s4-g4-t3','sftp','Verify the SSH subsystem and remote file permissions rather than assuming shell access is enough.',
'SFTP is a file-transfer protocol commonly carried as an SSH subsystem. It is distinct from FTP over TLS and inherits SSH transport/host/user security while applying server subsystem and filesystem authorization. A working interactive shell is not proof that the SFTP subsystem is enabled or that the user may read or write the intended path. File completion requires integrity and location evidence beyond login success.',
['Verify the expected SSH host identity and authenticate the intended user. The SFTP subsystem may be restricted independently of shell access. A subsystem-only account can transfer allowed files without an interactive shell.', 'Resolve the remote path under applicable chroot and filesystem permission policy. Client-visible paths can differ from host absolute paths. Check owner/group/ACL and allowed operations rather than granting broad write access to fix a single failure.', 'Transfer an owned disposable file, verify its length and independent hash at both ends, then remove only that file. A session opening or directory listing is not evidence that write, rename or final placement completed.'],
'Use a bounded transfer account and owned lab directory. Preserve subsystem/chroot/permissions before one negative path test. Verify approved read/write operations, intended denial and independent file integrity before declaring recovery.',
'Keep host verification enabled and private keys protected. Restrict paths and operations to intended scope; do not use an unrestricted administrative shell account merely to bypass a subsystem permission error.',
'SFTP commonly runs inside SSH on TCP22. It is not FTPS and does not use FTP control/data connections.',
['sftp -h','OpenSSH client; usage inspection only','Display supported syntax; this does not connect or transfer a file.','A usage display confirms local client availability only, not a working remote subsystem.'],[['OpenSSH manual pages','https://www.openssh.org/manual.html']],
[['SSH identity path accepted','Host verification and user authentication succeed under intended policy.','Do not bypass host trust merely to obtain a transfer connection.'],['Subsystem and path authorized','The SFTP subsystem and exact remote operation/path are allowed.','Shell access or directory listing does not establish write permission to the intended destination.'],['Transferred artifact verified','Destination path, length and independent content hash match.','Successful login or partial upload is not verified final file delivery.']],
[['SFTP and FTP over TLS the same protocol?','No.','SFTP commonly uses an SSH subsystem; FTPS retains FTP with TLS transport protections.'],['Shell works but SFTP fails; likely separate boundary?','Subsystem availability or authorization.','Remote shell and subsystem channels can have different policy and installation support.'],['Directory listing succeeds; destination write guaranteed?','No.','List, create, rename and write permissions are distinct filesystem operations.'],['Remote path seems absent under chroot; what context matters?','The client-visible filesystem root and permitted path.','Host absolute paths can differ from the namespace exposed to the transfer account.'],['Does SFTP require FTP passive data ports?','No.','Its file operations run through the SSH connection rather than FTP separate data sessions.'],['Partial file exists; complete transfer verified?','No; verify final length/hash and placement.','A created destination entry is weaker evidence than independent content integrity.'],['Must SFTP accounts have unrestricted shells?','No.','Subsystem-only restrictions can provide narrower transfer access where supported.'],['How verify permission recovery?','Restore intended permission and test owned artifact plus an intended denied path.','A broad chmod that makes everything writable would erase the security boundary.'],['What influences transfer time?','Storage, encryption, network and request behavior.','A negotiated SSH session does not measure sustained file throughput or endpoint storage capacity.'],['What cleanup is appropriate?','Remove only owned disposable files and restore precise path/account settings.','Broad directory deletion can remove unrelated data and is not a valid lab cleanup.']],['s4-g4-t2'])
add('s4-g4-t4','tftp','Track the initial request and negotiated transfer identifiers separately.',
'TFTP is a simple datagram file-transfer protocol with small operation semantics and no built-in user authentication or transport confidentiality. A client sends its initial request toward server UDP69; transfer messages then use the selected transfer identifiers, commonly UDP ports chosen for that exchange. Allowing only destination69 for every packet can break the actual data/acknowledgement flow. Simplicity does not make an image trustworthy.',
['Identify the owned filename and read or write operation. Server root and access policy constrain usable paths; directory listing and remote shell functions are not ordinary base TFTP capabilities. Never expose a permissive write root to untrusted networks.', 'Record the initial server port and selected client/server transfer identifiers. Data blocks and acknowledgements follow transfer context; packets from an unexpected TID must not be silently merged into the existing transfer.', 'Base block/acknowledgement progression and retries do not provide artifact authenticity. Verify expected size/hash from an independently trusted source after a lab transfer. Restore exact server-root/access settings and remove only owned test files.'],
'Limit TFTP to a tightly scoped disposable or otherwise controlled network need. Record negotiated tuple, filename, server root and expected artifact hash. Validate supported options and bootloader/device constraints before using a reference procedure for firmware.',
'TFTP lacks built-in identity and confidentiality. Use isolated access controls and independently trusted image signatures/hashes; do not place sensitive credentials or general writable storage behind it.',
'Initial requests commonly use UDP69; the actual transfer uses selected TIDs. It is incorrect to describe all TFTP traffic as a fixed69-to69 exchange.',
['tftp --help','Installed compatible lab client; availability/usage inspection only','Inspect the local client syntax without contacting a server.','Client presence and help output do not verify transfer tuple, server access or artifact authenticity.'],[rfc(1350)],
[['Initial request reaches intended root','The owned operation/filename maps to the allowed server root.','Reachability to UDP69 alone does not establish allowed file access.'],['Transfer identifiers accepted','The negotiated data/acknowledgement tuples are permitted and correlated.','A UDP69-only rule can admit requests but break later data packets.'],['Artifact independently verified','Final content matches a trusted expected length/hash or signature.','Completed transport alone does not authenticate the transferred image.']],
[['Are all TFTP packets addressed to UDP69?','No; only initiation conventionally targets69.','The exchange uses selected transfer identifiers for its subsequent data and acknowledgements.'],['Does base TFTP authenticate a user?','No.','Access control and artifact authenticity require protections outside the base protocol.'],['Request permitted but data blocked; first tuple check?','Negotiated client/server TIDs and direction.','A rule allowing only the initial server port can fail after a valid request.'],['File downloaded; firmware authenticity proved?','No.','Obtain trusted signature/hash evidence rather than trusting the unprotected transfer alone.'],['Can a packet from a different TID be assumed same transfer?','No.','Transfer identifier context prevents mixing unrelated datagrams into an existing exchange.'],['Does base TFTP offer a remote interactive shell?','No.','Its simple read/write transfer semantics differ from an administrative session protocol.'],['Should a writable root be exposed to an untrusted uplink?','No.','Unprotected file operations can permit tampering or data exposure without identity checks.'],['How verify restored transfer access?','Repeat the owned operation and independently verify the resulting artifact.','Listener availability alone misses negotiated data-path and content failures.'],['What limits simple block/ack progression?','Round-trip delay and supported block/window options affect transfer behavior.','No worksheet estimates actual bootloader throughput; feature support must be verified.'],['What is safe cleanup?','Delete only the owned test artifact and restore precise root/access policy.','Do not remove firmware or unrelated root files to clear a transfer error.']],['s4-g4-t3'])
add('s4-g4-t5','ftp','Separate FTP control login, data connection and transport protection.',
'FTP uses a control session and separate data connections. Conventional control is TCP21. In classic active mode the server initiates a data connection, conventionally from port20; passive mode lets the client connect to a server-advertised negotiated endpoint. A control login can succeed while listing or file transfer fails because the data path is blocked. Ordinary FTP exposes credentials and data; FTPS and SFTP are different protection designs.',
['Establish the intended control endpoint and authentication policy, then identify active/passive behavior and any negotiated address/port. Commands and reply codes on the control connection describe requested actions; they are not data delivery themselves.', 'In passive mode inspect the actual advertised endpoint, including NAT/address translation and allowed passive range. In active mode inspect the server-initiated data path to the client. Avoid opening broad unrelated port ranges as a troubleshooting shortcut.', 'For explicit FTPS, TLS negotiation and applicable data-channel protection are separate policy checks. SFTP is not the TLS form of FTP. Transfer an owned file, independently verify destination/hash and restore exact data-port and access policy.'],
'Prefer an appropriate protected transfer design over plain FTP for sensitive data. Preserve passive range, advertised address, TLS and filesystem settings before a lab fault. Correlate control reply, data tuple and final artifact from the same transaction.',
'Plain FTP credentials/content lack modern confidentiality. TLS support does not automatically mean the intended data channel is protected or server identity verified. Restrict accounts and do not expose broad anonymous write access.',
'FTP control conventionally TCP21; active data conventionally sourceTCP20 and passive data negotiated ports. FTPS can use explicit TLS on21 or deployment-specific implicit conventions.',
['curl --version','curl client; read-only feature inspection','Inspect advertised protocol/TLS features without performing a transfer.','Client features do not prove server TLS policy, data-channel connectivity or artifact delivery.'],[rfc(959)],
[['Control identity accepted','The intended control server/user policy is supplied as accepted.','A working control connection is not a completed data transfer.'],['Negotiated data path ready','The active/passive tuple and firewall/NAT context are correct.','A blocked data connection can prevent listings despite successful login.'],['Intended transfer verified','Owned destination content and transport-protection policy are verified.','An FTP completion reply alone does not replace independent integrity or channel-policy checks.']],
[['Control login works, listing hangs; which path next?','The separately negotiated data connection.','FTP control and data sessions can encounter different firewall/NAT policy.'],['Passive data always uses server port20?','No.','Passive mode advertises a negotiated server endpoint for the client to connect to.'],['SFTP simply means FTP with TLS?','No.','SFTP commonly uses SSH; FTPS protects the distinct FTP control/data design.'],['Passive reply advertises unreachable private address; inspect what?','Advertised endpoint and NAT/passive configuration.','A valid login does not make an unusable negotiated data address reachable.'],['Plain FTP protects credentials in transit?','No.','Ordinary FTP provides no modern encryption for credentials or content.'],['Explicit control TLS proves data confidentiality?','No; inspect the data-protection policy too.','Control and data protection choices must be verified under the intended FTPS configuration.'],['Classic active data direction?','The server initiates toward the client endpoint.','That direction differs from passive mode and can interact differently with client-side filtering.'],['How verify restored data path?','Repeat the original owned transfer and verify destination length/hash.','A successful control reconnect can leave the data-path problem unresolved.'],['Why bound passive port ranges?','To constrain operational policy and firewall exposure.','A documented range is easier to authorize and verify than indiscriminately opening all ports.'],['What records distinguish control from delivery?','Control replies, negotiated tuple, data result and independently verified artifact.','These separate observers avoid calling successful authentication a completed transfer.']],['s4-g4-t3'])
add('s4-g4-t6','http','Verify HTTP semantics and HTTPS identity in the original application request.',
'HTTP defines request/response semantics, methods, status codes and representation metadata. HTTPS protects HTTP using TLS and requires a client trust policy for server identity. DNS resolution, TCP/QUIC transport, certificate validation, HTTP status and application correctness are separate stages. HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 commonly use TCP; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP. A200 response can still contain an application error or unintended content.',
['Preserve the intended URL scheme, hostname, method, headers and observer. Name-based routing and certificate identity depend on the hostname, so replacing it with an IP can test a different service. Redirects may change the final endpoint and trust context.', 'Validate certificate chain, reference identity, validity time and applicable usage under the actual client policy. Do not disable verification to make a connection pass. A TLS handshake identifies a protected peer under policy; it does not authorize an application action.', 'Inspect status, headers, body and expected application result.401/403 are explicit authentication/authorization responses; a timeout is missing transaction evidence. Cache behavior, redirects and reverse proxies can affect which content a client sees.'],
'Define expected URL/method/result, server identity and authenticated user scope. Preserve proxy/cache/TLS settings before a disposable fault. Verify the original transaction and an intended denial after restore, including final endpoint and expected content.',
'Keep server certificate validation enabled, restrict credentials and avoid recording sensitive headers in shared evidence. Transport confidentiality does not prevent unsafe application input, authorization bugs or unexpected redirects.',
'HTTP conventionally TCP80; HTTPS TCP443 for HTTP1.1/2 and commonly UDP443 for HTTP3/QUIC. Ports alone do not identify application correctness.',
['curl --version','curl client; read-only capability inspection','Inspect supported protocols and TLS implementation without contacting an external service.','Local features are not proof of server identity, HTTP response or application success.'],[rfc(9110)],
[['Intended origin and route','URL hostname/method and observer represent the original request.','A changed hostname or proxy path can test a different application.'],['TLS identity validation accepted','The actual policy accepts chain, hostname, time and usage where applicable.','An open port or disabled verification is not authenticated HTTPS identity.'],['Expected application result observed','The specific status/content/action matches the test objective.','HTTP200 alone does not guarantee correct application behavior.']],
[['Does HTTP200 guarantee the expected application content?','No.','An error page or incorrect representation can be returned with a successful HTTP status.'],['HTTPS always uses TCP only?','No; HTTP3 uses QUIC over UDP.','HTTP version and negotiated transport are part of the observation, not just the URL scheme.'],['IP substituted for hostname; equivalent identity test?','Not necessarily.','Reference identity, SNI and virtual-host routing can change when the hostname changes.'],['Certificate mismatch fixed by disabling verification?','No.','That bypasses the intended identity check instead of repairing the certificate or requested name.'],['401 or403 same as a network timeout?','No.','They are explicit HTTP access responses; timeout lacks that application response evidence.'],['What must be tracked across redirects?','Final origin and identity/credential policy.','A redirect can move the transaction to a different endpoint and change security assumptions.'],['TLS authenticated server guarantees user authorized?','No.','Server identity and application user/action authorization are separate decisions.'],['What demonstrates recovery?','Original URL/method/user gets expected content with identity validation enabled.','Testing another URL or disabling security can hide the original failure.'],['Does shorter cache lifetime always improve delivery?','No; it increases origin dependency and request work.','Cache policy trades freshness and resource use and needs workload-specific evidence.'],['What belongs in a safe evidence record?','Redacted request context, TLS identity, final origin and expected response assertions.','Credentials and sensitive payloads should not be copied into a shared troubleshooting notebook.']],['s4-g0-t2','s4-g4-t2'])
# Trust, monitoring and integrated service evidence.
add('s4-g5-t0','pki','Build a trust path while keeping identity, key custody and authorization separate.',
'A public key infrastructure binds subject identities or attributes to public keys through signed certificates and a relying-party trust policy. A certification authority signs certificates; a relying party decides whether a path to a locally trusted anchor is acceptable for its intended use. An intermediate can sign subordinate certificates only under applicable constraints. A valid signature proves a key signed data, not that every signed identity should be trusted or authorized.',
['Identify the root trust anchor, intermediate issuers and endpoint leaf. A self-signed certificate is not automatically trusted: local trust policy is the starting authority. The private key is distinct from the distributable certificate and must remain protected.', 'Path validation checks signatures and constraints such as CA eligibility, validity and intended usage under the actual client policy. Issuer discovery alone is not a validated chain. Missing intermediates and untrusted roots are different faults with different repairs.', 'Plan issuance, renewal, revocation response and key compromise handling before deployment. Verify the exact service identity at the relying client, not merely a chain viewer. Restore owned trust changes without exporting private keys or trusting unrelated roots.'],
'Define approved issuers and relying-party trust stores, controlled key generation/custody, certificate inventory and renewal owners. Preserve a disposable trust store before adding a lab CA; remove only that owned anchor after verifying restoration.',
'A compromised private key requires incident handling beyond editing a certificate name. Protect CA keys more strongly than ordinary artifacts, constrain issuance and avoid indiscriminately importing roots to fix a validation error.',
'PKI is a trust framework with no single intrinsic port. Enrollment, revocation and protected application protocols use their own transports.',
['openssl version','OpenSSL lab client; version inspection only','Inspect the local cryptographic tool version without generating keys or changing trust.','Tool availability is not a validated certificate chain or an authenticated application endpoint.'],[rfc(5280)],
[['Anchor trusted by relying party','The declared trust anchor is permitted in the actual client policy.','Self-signature or a downloaded root does not automatically establish trust.'],['Path constraints valid','The supplied issuer chain meets signatures, CA and usage/time constraints.','A chain assembled from names alone does not demonstrate path validation.'],['Private key custody intact','The relevant private keys remain protected under intended ownership.','A valid public certificate does not repair disclosure of its private key.']],
[['Self-signed root automatically trusted?','No; trust is a relying-party policy choice.','A self-signature cannot establish an independent reason to trust its signer.'],['Certificate and private key interchangeable artifacts?','No.','Certificates distribute public bindings; private keys must remain confidential and protected.'],['Intermediate named as issuer means chain validated?','No.','Signatures, constraints and a path to a permitted trust anchor must be checked.'],['Leaf signature valid but anchor unknown; acceptance?','Not under that trust policy.','Cryptographic signature correctness and locally permitted trust are separate requirements.'],['Does a valid certificate authorize every action?','No.','Identity binding and application authorization remain distinct decisions.'],['Missing intermediate and untrusted root same fault?','No.','One concerns path construction; the other concerns the relying party trust decision.'],['Private key leaked; renew same key sufficient?','No; handle compromise and replace/revoke as policy requires.','A new validity period does not make a disclosed signing key secret again.'],['How verify a restored trust path?','Use the intended client policy and exact service identity, then test intended denial.','A generic viewer can apply a different trust store or omit application identity checks.'],['What threatens availability during renewal?','Unowned inventory, expiry gaps and incomplete deployed chains.','A certificate issued successfully is not necessarily deployed to every consuming endpoint.'],['What cleanup after a lab CA?','Remove the owned lab trust anchor and revoke/remove owned artifacts under the lab plan.','Broad trust-store replacement could damage unrelated endpoint validation.']],['s4-g4-t6'])
add('s4-g5-t1','certificates','Validate a certificate for the actual hostname, time and intended use.',
'A certificate contains a signed public-key binding and attributes such as issuer, subject alternative names, validity and usage constraints. Client acceptance depends on path trust and application-specific identity policy, not just whether a certificate file parses. For HTTPS, the requested reference hostname must satisfy the client identity check. Expiry, wrong hostname, unsuitable usage and unknown issuer are distinct failure causes.',
['Read issuer, subject/SAN, validity and extensions without exposing private material. Compare the actual requested hostname with supported reference-identity rules. A familiar organization name or common-name display is not sufficient evidence of hostname validation.', 'Check current client time and applicable key/extended-key usage along the validated path. An unexpired certificate is not necessarily trusted, usable for this service or associated with the intended endpoint. Server chain delivery and client trust stores can differ.', 'Revocation processing is policy- and implementation-dependent; do not assume every client checks every revocation source or fails identically when one is unreachable. Test intended validator behavior using owned fixtures and verify renewal deployment at the original service endpoint.'],
'Track certificate owner, names, issuer, renewal lead time and deployment targets. Preserve endpoint binding and trust configuration before one lab replacement. Verify the intended hostname with checks enabled and retain the precise failed validation reason.',
'Do not bypass hostname or expiry checks to hide a deployment fault. Protect private keys and issuance authority; certificate metadata can be public while its corresponding signing material must remain protected.',
'A certificate has no intrinsic port; HTTPS/SSH/enrollment protocols have their own transport conventions and validation rules.',
['openssl x509 -in labs/owned-server.pem -noout -issuer -subject -dates -ext subjectAltName','OpenSSL with a separately prepared owned PEM fixture; guided only','Inspect public certificate metadata without altering trust or reading a private key.','Parsing and metadata do not prove path trust, reference identity, revocation or a live endpoint.'],[rfc(5280)],
[['Requested identity matches','The intended reference name passes applicable certificate identity policy.','A valid certificate for another hostname remains wrong for this request.'],['Time and usage acceptable','The actual client clock and intended usage pass policy.','Unexpired alone does not establish suitable usage or correct client time.'],['Trust and revocation policy pass','The supplied path and configured revocation behavior are accepted.','Revocation checks vary; do not infer a universal check from a displayed certificate.']],
[['Unexpired certificate automatically valid for any hostname?','No.','Reference identity and trust must still match the requested service.'],['What field commonly carries HTTPS DNS identities?','Subject alternative name.','Inspect applicable reference-identity rules rather than assuming a friendly subject display is enough.'],['Client clock wrong; why can validation fail?','Validity is evaluated against the client time.','The certificate interval and observer clock jointly determine a time-based decision.'],['Parsing PEM succeeds; live TLS verified?','No.','File syntax and metadata do not establish endpoint presentation or relying-party validation.'],['Can unsuitable EKU still matter before expiry?','Yes.','Allowed key usage is an independent constraint for the intended service.'],['Every client necessarily performs identical revocation checks?','No.','Policy, implementation, caching and failure behavior vary and require actual validator evidence.'],['Issuer name matches known CA; path necessarily trusted?','No.','Names do not substitute for signatures, chain constraints and an approved trust anchor.'],['How verify renewal deployment?','Inspect and validate the certificate presented by the original endpoint/name.','An issued file can remain undeployed or attached to another listener.'],['Why alert before expiry rather than at expiry?','Renewal, approval and deployment need operational lead time.','Expiry detection without remediation ownership can create avoidable service outages.'],['What record helps repair a mismatch safely?','Requested name, presented SAN/chain, client time and exact validation error.','That context identifies the actual cause without disabling the identity checks.']],['s4-g5-t0'])
add('s4-g5-t2','dot1x','Separate endpoint EAP, authenticator decisions and policy-server transport.',
'802.1X controls network access through a supplicant, authenticator and authentication-server role. EAPOL carries local-link authentication exchanges between endpoint and access device; the authenticator commonly transports EAP to a RADIUS server. The controlled data port and authentication exchange have distinct states. EAP-TLS can prove certificate-based identities under configured policy, while MAC authentication bypass is a different and weaker identity mechanism.',
['Identify the supplicant and the actual access-device port. Authentication frames must be permitted through the uncontrolled authentication path while ordinary data access follows controlled-port policy. Link-up alone does not imply authorized data access.', 'The authenticator relays EAP under a configured policy-server relationship. Verify server identity in the supplicant policy and endpoint/client identity at the server as required by the EAP method. RADIUS reachability is not successful EAP validation.', 'Apply returned role/VLAN and separately test intended traffic. Critical-auth/fail-open and MAB fallback policies are explicit design choices, not evidence of successful EAP-TLS. Preserve independent management and test accept, reject, server outage and local recovery in an isolated access port.'],
'Define EAP method, supplicant trust, server client mapping, assigned access policy and explicit outage behavior. Preserve the exact lab-port policy and console management before one fault. Verify authorized/denied traffic and authentication records separately.',
'Do not disable server certificate validation in supplicants. MAC addresses can be copied; MAB does not provide the same identity assurance as properly configured EAP-TLS. Contain any fail-open exception and record it as a policy exception.',
'EAPOL is Layer2 and has no TCP/UDP port. Authenticator-to-server RADIUS commonly uses UDP1812, with accounting separately1813.',
['show authentication sessions interface GigabitEthernet1/0/10 details','Cisco IOS XE17 access device where supported; owned port placeholder','Inspect method, state and assigned policy on the intended lab port.','Authorized display must be corroborated with intended permitted/denied traffic and identity evidence.'],[rfc(9190)],
[['Intended EAP identity validated','The selected method and expected endpoint/server identities pass policy.','MAB or an outage exception is not successful EAP-TLS authentication.'],['Authenticator applies correct role','The consuming port installs the intended controlled access policy.','Server acceptance alone does not prove the desired VLAN or role.'],['Permitted and denied traffic verified','The owned tests corroborate actual intended access boundaries.','Link-up and an authorized label do not prove application policy enforcement.']],
[['Who connects supplicant EAP to the policy server?','The authenticator.','The access device mediates the local EAPOL exchange and commonly carries EAP through RADIUS.'],['EAPOL uses UDP1812 directly on the client link?','No; EAPOL is Layer2.','RADIUS transport belongs to the authenticator/server relationship rather than the endpoint EAPOL frame.'],['Link-up means controlled data port authorized?','No.','Carrier and identity-based data access are distinct port states.'],['MAB success equivalent to EAP-TLS identity proof?','No.','A copied MAC address has weaker identity assurance than validated certificate possession.'],['Should supplicant server validation be disabled for convenience?','No.','Otherwise endpoint credentials or identity can be exposed to an unintended authentication server.'],['Server Accept guarantees correct VLAN applied?','No.','The authenticator must support and install the returned policy on the intended port.'],['Critical-auth permits traffic during outage; authentication proved?','No; it is an explicit exception.','Fail-open behavior must be documented and tested rather than counted as EAP success.'],['How verify restored port policy?','Correct method/identity, role and intended allowed/denied traffic all pass.','A session label alone omits the actual data-access boundary.'],['What can cause repeated authentication churn?','Method/certificate/time or policy problems, not only link failure.','Correlate authentication causes and timing before treating every retry as a physical fault.'],['What must remain independent during the lab?','Console management and the saved original port policy.','Recovery must not depend on the experimental access authentication succeeding.']],['s4-g2-t1','s4-g5-t1'])
add('s4-g5-t3','monitoring','Define a measurable service objective and distinguish silence from healthy state.',
'Network monitoring gathers observations and evaluates conditions to support operational decisions. Availability, latency, loss, utilization and identity-policy outcomes describe different properties. A metric value is meaningful only with units, observer, interval and provenance. No-data can reflect collection failure, an absent object or actual inactivity; it must not automatically be rendered as zero or healthy. Alert delivery and response ownership are additional dependencies beyond detection.',
['Define the original service, client population and intended test before selecting signals. Polling device uptime, active probes, logs and flow summaries answer different questions. Combine user-facing checks and internal evidence without treating correlation as a proven cause.', 'Record sample interval, timestamp and missingness. Counters need rates and discontinuity context; gauges describe values at samples. An average can hide tail delay or brief loss, and a stale green value is not current evidence.', 'Define warning/critical duration, recovery threshold, no-data behavior and notification ownership. Test detection, notification and acknowledgement with an owned scenario, then verify recovery and suppressions. Dashboard visibility alone does not establish an effective response process.'],
'Assign metric definitions, owners, thresholds, dependency suppression and retention. Keep raw observations separate from inferred incident causes. Preserve alert policy before one lab outage/no-data test and verify expected firing, notification and resolution.',
'Restrict monitoring credentials and sensitive telemetry access. A collector is a valuable administrative target; use least privilege and log changes to thresholds and alert-routing policy.',
'Monitoring has no single intrinsic port; collectors, agents, queries and synthetic probes use their own protocols.',
['date -u','Linux read-only clock observation','Record observer wall time; this is not proof that the clock is synchronized.','Timestamp provenance helps interpretation but requires separate synchronization evidence.'],[['Prometheus overview','https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/']],
[['Signal matches service objective','The selected observation measures a documented service property.','A ping or uptime metric alone may not represent the original user workflow.'],['Samples are fresh and interpretable','Units, interval, missingness and clock provenance are recorded.','No-data or stale data must not silently become healthy zero.'],['Alert and response path tested','Condition, notification and owner acknowledgement are supplied as verified.','A visible dashboard is not a tested incident notification workflow.']],
[['Does no-data necessarily mean zero load?','No.','Collection failure or absent series can also explain missing samples.'],['Device uptime proves application available?','No.','A running device can host a failing service or broken authorization policy.'],['Why define observer and interval?','They determine what the measurement represents.','Values from different source paths or sampling windows cannot be compared as if equivalent.'],['Average latency low but users report delay; what else?','Tail distribution and representative client/path evidence.','Aggregates can hide the slow experiences relevant to the original service objective.'],['Metric correlates with outage; cause proved?','No.','Correlation guides hypotheses; intervention or independent causal evidence is still needed.'],['Counter raw value equals current rate?','No.','Rate needs a time interval, units and continuity assumptions.'],['Dashboard green but last sample old; healthy now?','Insufficient evidence.','Freshness is part of a status conclusion rather than an optional decoration.'],['How verify alert recovery?','Observed restoration, resolved condition and expected notification/owner behavior.','A cleared graph without confirmed response semantics is incomplete operational evidence.'],['What does higher cardinality cost?','More series storage, query and ingestion work.','Labels should serve decisions; per-request identifiers can create uncontrolled resource growth.'],['What should an alert rule specify?','Meaning, duration, no-data policy, owner and a tested response.','A threshold without ownership and failure behavior is a number rather than an operational plan.']],['s4-g3-t2','s4-g1-t1'])
add('s4-g6-t0','prtg','Map a sensor to its probe, target and notification dependency.',
'PRTG organizes monitoring around objects such as devices and sensors, with probes performing checks and a core handling data and user-facing management. A sensor represents a particular check or measurement, not an entire device guarantee. Probe reachability, target credentials, individual sensor behavior and notification triggers are separate dependencies. Product editions, licensing, supported sensor types and sizing guidance can change; verify them for the chosen release before deployment.',
['Identify the probe that runs the sensor and the exact target/interface/service it observes. A central server reaching a host does not establish that a remote probe has the same route or credentials. Sensor requirements differ by protocol and operating environment.', 'Read sensor status, channel units, interval and last data/error. Inherited settings and dependencies can pause/suppress observations; a paused sensor is not a measured healthy service. Avoid counting missing data as a successful transaction.', 'Test a harmless owned sensor failure, notification trigger and recovery. Compare probe result, central status and delivered notification. Verify edition/license and resource sizing against current vendor guidance rather than prescribing a universal sensor allowance.'],
'Plan probe placement, sensor definition, least-privilege credentials, inheritance and dependencies. Preserve a test sensor and notification policy before one outage scenario; restore it and verify fresh results plus notification recovery.',
'Protect probe/core credentials and restrict administrative UI/API access. Do not reuse broad domain-administrator privileges for a narrowly scoped check; redact sensor outputs containing identity or endpoint details.',
'Target-check ports depend on the sensor; probe/core and UI connections are product configuration. No universal port proves all PRTG checks.',
['Open the owned sensor: Overview → latest result/error; inspect its probe and inherited settings.','PRTG prepared lab UI; guided not executed','Read the actual observer, interval, channels and dependencies; no installation is performed.','Sensor status is scoped to that check, and notification delivery needs separate receiver evidence.'],[['PRTG manual','https://www.paessler.com/manuals/prtg']],
[['Intended probe can run check','Probe location and required credentials/support match the sensor.','Core reachability alone does not establish the remote probe path.'],['Sensor reports fresh intended data','The check is running with correct units and current results.','Paused or stale sensor state is not a fresh healthy measurement.'],['Notification workflow verified','Trigger, recipient and recovery behavior are corroborated.','A red sensor does not prove an alert reached its responsible operator.']],
[['A sensor represents universal device health?','No; it is a particular check.','Device-wide conclusions require multiple scoped observations and an explicit service objective.'],['Core reaches host, remote probe necessarily can?','No.','Probe routing, filtering and credentials can differ from the central server.'],['Paused sensor means target healthy?','No.','A paused check supplies no fresh health measurement under that pause condition.'],['Red sensor but no email; inspect what?','Notification triggers, dependencies and delivery evidence.','Detection and notification are separate operational paths.'],['Inherited credentials irrelevant to a child sensor?','No.','Inheritance can determine the identity used by a check and must be inspected at its consumer.'],['Every sensor type supported identically in every edition?','No.','Release/edition/environment support and licensing must be checked in current vendor material.'],['Ping sensor proves protected web content correct?','No.','An echo measurement has narrower semantics than an authenticated application request.'],['How verify restored monitoring?','Fresh intended sensor data plus notification/recovery behavior.','A probe-online label alone misses check-specific and recipient failures.'],['Can a universal sensor count guarantee sizing?','No.','Check type, interval, infrastructure and current vendor guidance affect resource requirements.'],['What record belongs with a sensor?','Probe, target, check/units, credentials scope, interval, owner and dependencies.','Those fields make a sensor result operationally interpretable rather than a generic status light.']],['s4-g5-t3'])
add('s4-g6-t1','zabbix','Follow an item value through trigger evaluation to an owned action.',
'Zabbix collects values through configured items and evaluates trigger expressions that can create events and invoke actions. Agents, SNMP checks and other item types observe different properties. A proxy can collect and buffer data in a remote context before passing it to the server. A current item value, trigger problem and delivered action are distinct stages; an accessible frontend does not prove any of them.',
['Identify host, item key/type, units, interval and actual collection endpoint. Passive and active agent designs differ in initiation and configuration. Unsupported item state or missing data is not automatically a zero measurement.', 'Evaluate the actual trigger expression, time window and dependencies against collected data. Maintenance and suppression can alter event/notification handling. A host ping should not be substituted for an application item simply to obtain a green display.', 'Follow a problem event through action conditions, recipient permissions and delivery. Proxy buffering can delay visibility; preserve event/data timestamps. Test one owned item fault and restoration, then verify trigger resolution and action behavior.'],
'Plan template inheritance, host identities, item support, proxy placement and response ownership. Preserve item/trigger/action settings before one lab fault. Verify observed data and configured expressions under the actual release instead of assuming template names prove correctness.',
'Limit agent access and remote-command features, protect server/proxy credentials and use supported transport protection. Control frontend privileges and avoid exposing secret-bearing item data in shared screenshots.',
'Conventional agent TCP10050 and server/proxy TCP10051 depend on passive/active configuration; SNMP and application checks use their own transports.',
['Open Latest data for the owned item, then inspect its trigger event and action history.','Zabbix prepared lab UI; guided not executed','Trace item timestamps, evaluation and actual recipient delivery independently.','Frontend login and host presence do not prove current item collection or delivered notifications.'],[['Zabbix manual introduction','https://www.zabbix.com/documentation/current/en/manual/introduction']],
[['Item collection valid','Configured host/item type, observer and supported key match.','An unsupported item can have no valid value even while the host is reachable.'],['Trigger uses current intended data','Expression, window and missing-data semantics match the objective.','An old value or wrong expression can conceal the original condition.'],['Action delivery corroborated','Matching event/action and responsible recipient evidence are present.','A problem event alone does not prove the notification action completed.']],
[['Item and trigger the same object?','No.','An item collects a value; a trigger evaluates a condition over data.'],['Frontend reachable proves collection healthy?','No.','UI access is distinct from agent, proxy, server and item processing.'],['Unsupported item should be treated as zero?','No.','Unsupported or missing data needs explicit handling and is not a valid numeric sample.'],['Problem appears but recipient gets nothing; next stage?','Action conditions, permissions and delivery history.','Detection and notification have independently configured dependencies.'],['Proxy buffering can affect which observation?','When central data/events become visible.','Preserve sample and receive times to distinguish collection from transfer delay.'],['Active/passive agent initiation identical?','No.','Their connection direction and configuration requirements differ.'],['Template attached proves all items meaningful?','No.','Actual supported keys, targets, credentials and data must be verified at the host.'],['How demonstrate restored alerting?','Fresh item values, correct trigger resolution and intended action behavior.','A host availability icon alone omits the item-to-event-to-recipient chain.'],['Why tune item interval and history retention?','They affect detection granularity, collection and storage work.','More samples are not automatically more useful or sustainable.'],['What must a fault record name?','Host, item key/type, timestamp, trigger expression, event and action.','Scope prevents a generic host state being mistaken for a specific service measurement.']],['s4-g5-t3'])
add('s4-g6-t2','librenms','Separate inventory discovery, successful polling and retained time-series evidence.',
'LibreNMS provides network-device discovery, polling, historical graphs and alerting with supported device/OS integrations. Discovery can identify a device and its objects, while polling obtains current values and storage maintains history. A discovered hostname does not prove successful authenticated polling or meaningful graph continuity. Supported modules and field interpretation differ by device OS and product version.',
['Identify the actual device OS support, management endpoint and SNMP identity/view. Discovery populates inventory and object mapping; missing or misidentified interfaces can affect later graphs even if the device responds.', 'Inspect poller execution, last poll time and errors along with instance/counter mapping. A graph gap can result from poll failure, storage failure or a changed instance. Correlate device-side management evidence before calling every empty graph a traffic outage.', 'Follow retained values and alert rules to recipient evidence. Distributed polling and storage design must match the chosen deployment. Test an owned view fault, restore it and verify fresh values and continuity without resetting unrelated history.'],
'Plan supported device inventory, SNMPv3 credentials/views, poller placement, storage and alerts. Preserve exact device settings and graph mapping before one controlled fault. Confirm OS support and deployment prerequisites using current documentation.',
'Use least-privilege management access and restrict poller/UI credentials. Inventory and graphs disclose topology and traffic patterns. A successful discovery record is not endpoint authentication or permission to modify the device.',
'SNMP polling conventionally UDP161; notification and UI transports are separate. Distributed poller/storage connections depend on deployment.',
['Inspect the owned device last poll/error and compare interface instance mapping with graph timestamps.','LibreNMS prepared lab UI; guided not executed','Read discovery, polling and storage evidence as distinct stages.','A discovered device or graph frame does not establish fresh values or alert delivery.'],[['LibreNMS documentation','https://docs.librenms.org/']],
[['Device objects mapped correctly','Supported OS and intended OID/instance mapping are known.','Wrong discovery or instance mapping can mislabel later values.'],['Poller obtains fresh values','The intended observer and credentials yield current data.','A discovered device can still fail actual polling.'],['History and alerts corroborated','Retained samples and intended alert delivery are observed.','Fresh polling alone does not establish stored history or recipient delivery.']],
[['Discovered hostname proves current polling?','No.','Inventory discovery and repeated value collection are separate operations.'],['SNMP success means every interface mapped correctly?','No.','OS support and OID/instance interpretation still need verification.'],['Empty graph necessarily zero traffic?','No.','Missing polling or storage data can explain the gap.'],['Device responds, last poll stale; inspect what?','Poller scheduling, credentials/view and error evidence.','Reachability does not prove the collector obtained the selected objects.'],['Changing instance index can affect history?','Yes.','Different object mapping can disconnect or mislabel comparisons across intervals.'],['Every network OS equally supported?','No.','Discovery and polling behavior depend on maintained integrations and actual version compatibility.'],['Fresh values automatically prove alert delivery?','No.','Alert rule and recipient paths are separate from data collection.'],['How verify restored view?','Poll intended objects, check fresh timestamps/history and intended denied scope.','Widening all access can erase least privilege while making a graph populate.'],['What affects poller capacity?','Device/object count, check cost, interval and storage design.','A static device count does not establish sustainable collection latency.'],['What evidence isolates a graph gap?','Instance mapping, poll timestamps/errors and storage continuity.','Those records distinguish missing observation from actual zero load.']],['s4-g1-t0','s4-g5-t3'])
add('s4-g6-t3','grafana','Trace a displayed panel to its query, data source and alert failure policy.',
'Grafana queries configured data sources and presents results through panels, dashboards and supported alert evaluation. It is not inherently the collector that scraped every displayed metric. A panel depends on data-source connectivity/permissions, query/variables/time range and incoming data semantics. A beautiful empty chart can indicate no matching series or a failed query rather than healthy infrastructure.',
['Identify data source, query language, selected variables, time range and timezone. The displayed aggregation can hide tail behavior or combine unlike series. Match units and sample freshness to the documented measurement objective.', 'Distinguish query error, no-data and numeric zero. Panel transformations and overrides can change display without changing stored source data. A datasource connection test does not establish that the intended query returns meaningful current results.', 'Alert rules have their own evaluation, pending duration and no-data/error policy and may not reproduce a viewer-specific panel context. Test an owned query result, an empty/error case and recipient recovery. Preserve data-source/alert settings and restore the exact query.'],
'Define datasource access, panel query/unit/time range, alert semantics and recipient owner. Avoid exporting dashboards with secrets. Verify the actual data and an independent source query before interpreting panel colors as measured service health.',
'Restrict datasource credentials and dashboard/alert edit rights. A viewer should not gain broad backend query privileges merely to open one panel; inspect supported authorization behavior for the actual deployment.',
'Grafana UI port and backend data-source transports are configurable. It has no single port that proves all displayed data or alert delivery.',
['Inspect panel query and data inspection, then compare alert evaluation and notification history.','Grafana prepared lab UI; guided not executed','Record datasource, variables, units, time range and no-data/error policy.','A rendered panel or datasource test alone is not verified measurement or recipient delivery.'],[['Grafana introduction','https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/introduction/']],
[['Datasource query authorized','The intended backend connection and query permissions work.','A loaded dashboard shell does not establish datasource access.'],['Query represents current objective','Variables, units, interval and returned samples match the intended measurement.','An empty selection or stale series must not be mistaken for healthy zero.'],['Alert failure policy and delivery tested','No-data/error behavior and recipient recovery are corroborated.','A panel threshold color is not automatically the alert rule or notification result.']],
[['Grafana necessarily scrapes every shown metric itself?','No.','It commonly queries data held by configured backend sources.'],['Empty panel equivalent to zero utilization?','No.','No matching data and numeric zero have different semantics.'],['Datasource test passes; intended panel guaranteed useful?','No.','Query variables, time range, permissions and series semantics still matter.'],['Panel and alert necessarily use identical viewer context?','No.','Alert evaluation has its own configuration and supported query context.'],['Wrong panel unit changes backend stored values?','Not inherently.','Display transformations and source storage are distinct layers.'],['Should an alert no-data policy be left implicit?','No.','Collection failure can otherwise become silent or misleading status.'],['Low average panel proves every client fast?','No.','Aggregated views can hide tail delay or underrepresented users.'],['How verify restoration?','Correct original query/data plus expected alert/error/recovery delivery.','Changing to a different datasource or ignoring query errors can hide the original fault.'],['What influences query cost?','Time range, series cardinality, aggregation and backend capability.','Dashboard appearance does not certify backend resource use or responsiveness.'],['What makes a panel reviewable?','Datasource/query, units, variables, time range and documented interpretation.','Those details establish what a value represents instead of relying on a color legend.']],['s4-g5-t3'])
add('s4-g6-t4','prometheus','Interpret scrape success, counter rates and label cardinality separately.',
'Prometheus collects time-series data, commonly by scraping configured targets, and stores samples indexed by metric name and labels. PromQL evaluates expressions over that data; alerting rules and notification handling add further stages. The up metric indicates scrape success for a target under that scrape context, not the health of every application workflow. Unbounded labels such as per-request identifiers can create harmful series cardinality.',
['Define the target endpoint, scrape interval and supported exposition. A successful scrape can contain an application failure metric; an unsuccessful scrape differs from a successful sample whose gauge is zero. Preserve target labels and timestamp/freshness context.', 'Counters accumulate and can reset; rate over a suitable range interprets increments with supported reset handling. Gauges describe current values. Never subtract unrelated label sets or treat a raw monotonically accumulated counter as current per-second load.', 'Use bounded label dimensions, retention and rule evaluation that fit resource limits. Test an owned scrape failure, no-data condition and application-failure metric separately. Verify notification delivery and recovery, not merely that an expression returns a number.'],
'Define target ownership, scrape identity/access, metric types, labels, query range and alert response. Preserve scrape/rule configuration before a lab fault. Validate under the installed product release without assuming a universal retention or capacity recommendation.',
'Protect metrics and management endpoints; exported labels can disclose topology or user information. Keep secrets out of labels and restrict destructive administrative interfaces. Scrape access is not application authorization.',
'Prometheus HTTP listen port is commonly9090 but configurable; scraped exporter/application endpoints use their own configured ports. No port alone proves a meaningful series.',
['Inspect the owned target page, up{job="lab"}, and the documented service metric over a bounded interval.','Prometheus prepared lab UI; guided not executed','Compare scrape health, metric type/labels, sample freshness and alert evidence.','up=1 means that scrape succeeded, not that the original application transaction succeeded.'],[['Prometheus overview','https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/']],
[['Target scrape succeeds','The intended target endpoint returns an accepted scrape.','Scrape failure differs from a collected metric value of zero.'],['Metric semantics are correct','Type, labels, interval and reset context match the query.','A raw counter is not a current rate; unrelated series cannot be subtracted safely.'],['Service objective and alert verified','The application-specific signal and intended response are corroborated.','up=1 does not guarantee user-visible service success.']],
[['up=1 proves authenticated application workflow healthy?','No.','It establishes a successful scrape for that target, not all service properties.'],['Raw counter value equals requests per second?','No.','Rate needs an interval and appropriate counter/reset semantics.'],['No series and gauge zero interchangeable?','No.','Missing data and an observed zero must be handled separately.'],['Application error metric positive while up=1; contradictory?','No.','A scrape can succeed while faithfully reporting application failures.'],['Why avoid request ID as an unrestricted label?','It can create unbounded series cardinality.','Each unique label combination consumes collection/storage/query resources.'],['Counter reset means negative request rate?','Not under correct counter-aware rate interpretation.','Resets require supported semantics rather than a naive arithmetic subtraction.'],['Are scrape and notification success identical?','No.','Rule evaluation and recipient delivery are later independent stages.'],['How verify restored collection?','Fresh same-target samples plus application-specific and alert recovery evidence.','A target page alone omits the actual service metric and response workflow.'],['What trades off in a shorter scrape interval?','Detection granularity against collection/storage work.','More frequent samples do not automatically remove blind spots or improve service correctness.'],['What should a query record specify?','Metric type, labels, range, units, reset/missingness interpretation and objective.','An expression result needs provenance to support an operational conclusion.']],['s4-g5-t3'])
add('lab-4','services-lab','Correlate time, logging, polling and administrative policy without losing local recovery.',
'The Network Services Lab integrates the original objective: centralize NTP, Syslog, SNMP and AAA, verify timestamps, log delivery, polling, administrator authentication and a local recovery account. A guided disposable topology uses one owned managed device or service VM, separate service endpoints and an independent console. This browser rehearsal does not run those servers. It checks whether independently recorded prerequisites are complete, and deliberately refuses to infer central-service health from configuration alone.',
['Establish a secure baseline and independent console-local administrative account first. Record exact versions, addresses, source contexts, management policy and original settings. Choose one approved identity and one intentionally denied action; do not use a production device for policy fault injection.', 'Verify selected time source and client synchronization, then correlate an owned log marker at originator and collector with event/receive timestamps. Poll one permitted SNMP object under the intended security/view and verify an outside-view denial. Keep these observations separate instead of calling all three successful because the device answers ping.', 'Verify approved administrator authentication, intended command denial and retained accounting. Introduce only an owned central-server unavailability scenario while preserving console access; distinguish timeout recovery from explicit rejection. Restore exact settings and verify the original transactions and local recovery before removing owned test resources.'],
'Use the downloadable workbook as the evidence record. For every service keep baseline, one fault, recovery, observer/time and remaining limits. The completion boundary requires all six original observations; optional DNS/PKI/monitoring dependencies deepen the exercise without replacing NTP/Syslog/SNMP/AAA.',
'Do not expose secrets in screenshots or configuration exports. Preserve console recovery, least-privilege polling and intended rejection. A fallback that grants a rejected identity access is a policy failure even if the administrator can log in.',
'NTP commonly UDP123, Syslog transport per configured policy, SNMP polling UDP161, AAA according to chosen RADIUS/TACACS+ transport. Independent console recovery has no dependency on those central ports.',
['show ntp associations\nshow logging\nshow snmp user\nshow aaa servers','Cisco IOS XE17 supported disposable lab device; guided observations','Collect separate time, local-log, security-user and AAA-peer observations, then corroborate each at its intended receiver/client.','These displays alone do not complete synchronization, remote marker delivery, scoped OID access, user/command decisions or local recovery.'],[rfc(5905),rfc(5424),rfc(3411),rfc(8907)],
[['Time and telemetry evidence complete','Synchronization, correlated collector marker and scoped SNMP observation are independently recorded.','Ping and configuration displays cannot replace actual service observations.'],['AAA acceptance and rejection verified','Approved identity/action and intended denial have correlated evidence.','A rejection bypassed by fallback is a failure of the intended boundary.'],['Independent recovery and restore verified','Console-local recovery remains usable and original transactions pass after exact restore.','Central outage testing is unsafe without independently verified recovery.']],
[['What must be tested before central AAA changes?','The independent console-local recovery account.','Recovery must not depend on the experimental authentication service remaining reachable.'],['Ping succeeds; all original services verified?','No.','Time synchronization, log delivery, OID access and administrative decisions need their own observations.'],['Local log marker enough for centralized logging?','No; find its correlated retained record at the collector.','Origin generation and receiver ingestion/storage are separate evidence points.'],['Approved login succeeds but forbidden command allowed; pass?','No.','Identity acceptance must coexist with intended authorization denial.'],['Server Reject followed by local permit; safe outage recovery?','No.','Explicit rejection must not be treated as unreachability to bypass intended policy.'],['What SNMP negative test is needed?','An intended outside-view object denial.','Successful polling alone can conceal an overly broad management view.'],['What time record helps compare logs?','Client synchronization plus event/receive timestamps and timezone.','Formatted timestamp text without provenance can mislead correlation.'],['What proves complete restore?','Original time/log/poll/admin/denial transactions and independent recovery verified.','Restored configuration text alone does not show the original services work again.'],['Why inject one fault at a time?','To preserve a testable causal hypothesis and bounded recovery.','Simultaneous policy/path changes make negative outcomes ambiguous and rollback harder.'],['What must the final lab notebook separate?','Actual output, synthetic declarations, guided procedures and untested limits.','A browser rehearsal is educational evidence and must not be relabelled as executed central services.']],['s4-g0-t3','s4-g1-t0','s4-g1-t1','s4-g2-t0','s4-g5-t3'])
